\title{An Archival ALMA Search for Unresolved Spectral-Carrier
Technosignatures toward \NSystems{} Stellar Systems within 40 Parsecs}

\author{Glenn J. White\altaffilmark{1,2} and Robin Dey\altaffilmark{3}}
\altaffiltext{1}{School of Physical Sciences, The Open University, Walton Hall, Milton Keynes, MK7 6AA, UK; glenn.white@open.ac.uk}
\altaffiltext{2}{RAL Space, STFC Rutherford Appleton Laboratory, Harwell Campus, Didcot, OX11 0QX, UK}
\altaffiltext{3}{VBRL Holdings, 50/5 Huay Kaew Road, Chang Phueak, Chiang Mai, Thailand}

\begin{abstract}
We have searched the public archive of the Atacama Large
Millimeter/submillimeter Array (ALMA) for artificial narrowband radio
emission from nearby stars. ``Narrowband'' means narrow compared with an ALMA channel,
\ChanLoA--\ChanHiA\,kHz, not hertz-scale resolution. The primary experiment seeks an
unresolved drifting carrier in \NWinA{} fine-channel windows toward
\NSysClassA{} systems. Coverage is quoted per system, which is what a
limit on one star rests on: a median \SysUnionAMed\,GHz per system and
\UnionClassA\,GHz of unique sky frequency in all. A secondary
search of \NWinB{} coarse windows adds \UnionBonly\,GHz but cannot
discriminate drift.

Sensitivity is measured by injection through the complete selection. On each system's best window that selection
recovers a carrier nine times in ten above an equivalent isotropic
radiated power of $\PromoteSysMedA$\,W at the median system, over
$\PromoteSysLoA$--$\PromoteSysHiA$\,W. This is an upper bound: the
window-to-window transfer of that calibration is uncertain by
$\times\StratTransferNineLo$--$\times\StratTransferNineHi$ and dominates
the budget.

\textbf{We identify no independently confirmed narrowband carrier.}
\NStageOneWin{} windows were flagged by a screen comparing the star with
\NCtrl{} controls in the same field. The screen prioritises
candidates and is not a significance test: the stellar rank is not
exchangeable. \NStageOneLine{} are circumstellar or
foreground carbon monoxide, including the $\beta$~Pictoris disc recovered
blind. The other \NStageOneUnattrib{} have no
identification; none is a point source at the stellar position in the
visibilities, and none recurs.

Repetition is itself selected: only \EpIndep{} of \EpTot{} systems have
epochs more than a day apart, \EpYears{} spanning years, so an intermittent
emitter is weakly constrained. The sample is the ALMA archive-selected one
within 40\,pc, not the solar neighbourhood; with \NMSearched{} of
\NMCensus{} catalogued M~dwarfs covered it cannot constrain the local
habitable-zone planet population. No independent re-implementation
exists, so this result rests on internal validation alone.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{extraterrestrial intelligence, radio lines: stars, submillimetre: stars, surveys, astrobiology}

\enlargethispage{0\baselineskip}
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

A technological civilisation, signalling deliberately or going about
its affairs, may imprint the electromagnetic spectrum detectably,
a \emph{technosignature} \citep{Sheikh2020,SETIreview2025}.
Six decades of radio searches cover many thousands of stars yet sample only
\HaystackFrac{} of the haystack of transmitter frequency, bandwidth, power and
duty cycle \citep{Wright2018}. Three gaps persist. The millimetre and
submillimetre regime is very sparsely searched, and everything above that
axis's ${\sim}115$\,GHz upper limit more sparsely still.
Most surveys are aimed at interest-selected classes of star, leaving the local
stellar population unsampled as a population. Few reprocess \emph{existing}
interferometric archives.

This paper addresses all three, asking how strongly existing ALMA
observations constrain unresolved artificial spectral emission from
nearby stellar systems. To be unambiguous about the signal
searched: we look for an intrinsically narrow coherent carrier whose
intrinsic linewidth is unresolved by the native ALMA spectral channel, and
we do not claim hertz-scale spectral resolution. The fine channels used
here are \ChanLoA--\ChanHiA\,kHz wide, orders of magnitude broader than
a centimetre-wave narrowband backend, so we write ``unresolved narrowband
carrier'' below wherever ambiguity could arise.  We mine the
archive, correcting for proper motion and Doppler drift, for unresolved
spectral carriers (channel-confined excess at native ALMA resolution) and
anomalous millimetre continuum. Throughout, ``narrowband'' describes the
assumed intrinsically narrow transmitter and never the resolving power of
the data (\S\ref{sec:method}). Channelisation sets which of two search classes
a window belongs to, and the completeness of each class is measured in
\S\ref{sec:dwell}: \ClassBPct{} per cent of the searched windows carry no
drift discrimination at all, and only \NWinA{} support the fine-channel search
that ``narrowband SETI'' normally means (\S\ref{sec:method}).
Three contributions follow. The first is the systematic exploitation of
archival ALMA stellar fields, \NStarBands{} target/band datasets mined from
public data with no new observing time. The second is spatial-control screen
calibration for interferometric SETI, in which the same statistic is evaluated
at the star and at \NCtrl{} control positions so that the false-alarm rate is
measured and not assumed. The third is a quantitative accounting of the
frequency, power and drift domain actually searched, which gives the
non-detection explicit boundaries. No ancillary analysis carries a technosignature disposition. One worked case
runs through the paper, a crossing towards $\beta$~Pictoris, which is the
largest star-versus-control contrast here and is attributed to the disc's
known circumstellar CO across two transitions and
\NStageOneBpicEb{} execution blocks
(\S\ref{sec:technosearch}). Figure~\ref{fig:funnel} is the roadmap: it gives
the selection from catalogue to searched sample on the left, and the decision
chain from window to disposition on the right.

Two properties of the design bear on how every number below should be
read, and both are developed where they belong rather than argued here.
The statistic and the molecular-line mask were defined on these data, so
the rank probabilities are exploratory rather than pre-registered in any
conventional sense; what we claim is narrower and is set out with its
repository timestamps and its out-of-sample test in
\S\ref{sec:statistic} and \S\ref{sec:heldout}. And the statistic
compares a single position with single positions, which rules out the
obvious region-maximum alternative for reasons of exchangeability;
both forms are scored for every window and released, and the comparison is
audited in Appendix~\ref{sec:aumic}.

In plain terms: we looked for a radio transmitter's carrier signal in
every millimetre-wave observation ALMA has made public of a star within
40 parsecs, about 130 light years. We found no signal that repeats or
that can be confirmed independently. Thirteen features passed the first
automated cut; nine are carbon monoxide gas around or in front of the
stars, and the remaining four are no more numerous than chance predicts
and show no sign, in the raw interferometer data, of coming from the star
itself. The main limitations are that these observations were taken for
other purposes, so the stars are not a representative sample, and that
most of them were observed only once, so a signal that came and went
could not have been confirmed.

We adopt one convention for the whole paper: \textbf{wherever this paper
says ``sensitivity'' without naming another quantity, it means
$P_{90}^{\rm sel}$.} Four power scales appear below and only that last one
is a sensitivity: the trigger level $P_{\rm trig}$, the power
$P_{\rm eff}$ a transmitter must radiate to reach it, the trigger's own
90 per cent recovery $P_{90}$, and $P_{90}^{\rm sel}$, the power the
\emph{complete} automated selection recovers nine times in ten.
$P_{90}^{\rm sel}$ is the survey sensitivity and the only one of
the four quoted in the abstract, the discussion and the conclusions;
Appendix~\ref{app:notation} defines all of them and every other term used
here in a particular sense.

Throughout, the signal class this survey addresses is an
\emph{unresolved spectral-carrier technosignature}: emission confined to a
small number of spectral channels, spatially unresolved at the stellar
position on the relevant baselines, and persistent enough to recur between
epochs. Each of the three qualifiers is a real restriction and the title
carries them deliberately. A carrier broader than the searched channel
width, one that fills the synthesised beam, or one that appears in a single
epoch and never again would not be recovered by the selection described
here, whatever its power. The exclusion limits below apply to that class
and to no other.

The targets were not chosen for a technosignature search. They are the stars that archival disc and
planet-formation programmes happened to observe, so the sample is
enriched in F and G stars and depleted in M dwarfs relative to the
40\,pc census, and only \NMSearched{} of \NMCensus{} catalogued M~dwarfs
within 40\,pc appear in it at all. Every statistical statement below
therefore concerns \emph{the ALMA archive-selected stellar sample within
40\,pc} and not the solar neighbourhood; in particular the survey cannot
constrain the local habitable-zone planet population, which M~dwarfs
dominate (\S\ref{sec:bias}).





\section{Background}
\label{sec:background}

Radio searches, from \citet{CocconiMorrison1959} and \citet{Drake1960} through
the programmes reviewed by \citet{Tarter2001} and Project Phoenix
\citep{Backus2002}, divide into wide-field commensal and targeted surveys
\citep{Zhang2020,Czech2021,Morrison2023,FAST3IATLAS2025,LOFARdualsite2023},
with wide-field low-frequency coverage from the MWA \citep{Tremblay2024} and a
single-dish C- and K-band programme at 6 and 18\,GHz with the Sardinia
Radio Telescope \citep{SRT2025},
with machine-learning interference rejection now central
\citep{Ma2023,Choza2024,GLOBULAR2025,AnomalyParkesGBT2025,BRaTs2026}, a
capability our classical threshold search lacks. Essentially all of that work
lies below ${\sim}10$\,GHz, and the millimetre regime above it has been
searched only once before the present work, with ALMA \citep{Mason2024}. There are physical reasons to go higher: interstellar scattering and
dispersion fall steeply with frequency, a given aperture delivers a tighter
beam per watt of EIRP, and directional communication, radar and beamed power
are already engineered as coherent mm-wave emission. Atmospheric transmission,
receiver noise and phase stability raise the cost without forbidding it.

\citet{Mason2024} searched archival Band~3 windows toward 28 ``stellar
bycatch'' stars, observed for other science and usable for free,
placing EIRP$_{\rm min} > 7\times10^{17}$\,W for the nearest and
identifying the challenges of high-frequency work: drift rates scale
with frequency, smearing erodes sensitivity without a drift search, and
the dense forest of molecular lines raises the confusion background. A pilot study by one of us
(\citealt{White2026}; submitted, and not yet available to the reader)
extended that methodology to TRAPPIST-1 across Bands 3, 6 and 7 and
returned no detection. Nothing in the present paper rests on it: the
ranked target list it produced is superseded by the frozen work list of
\S\ref{sec:sample}, which is released in full, and every window here was
re-reduced and re-searched from the raw archive data with the pipeline of
\S\ref{sec:method}. Where a star appears in both, TRAPPIST-1 included,
the numbers to cite are this paper's frozen products.

Figure~\ref{fig:context} locates this survey in the
\emph{parameter space} of published searches, and it is not a sensitivity
comparison. The vertical coordinates of the different programmes
are not comparable completeness limits: each is its own threshold
statistic, evaluated over channel widths that differ by seven orders of
magnitude, and we know of no way to convert them to a common completeness.
What the figure shows is that ALMA occupies a different combination of
frequency and spectral resolution from cm-wave SETI, not that it is more or
less sensitive.

Where a numerical comparison is possible it should be stated plainly rather
than read off the figure. Essentially all previous narrowband
technosignature work lies below ${\sim}10$\,GHz, the upper bound of the
Breakthrough Listen L- and C-band surveys and of the
\citet{Wright2018} haystack axis, so a search spanning
\UnionLo--\UnionHi\,GHz moves the searched frequency range up by about
two orders of magnitude relative to that boundary. Any EIRP advantage
toward the nearest systems is a $d^{2}$ effect and not an instrumental one:
\S\ref{sec:null} decomposes it. This survey samples far fewer stars, far
fewer epochs and a channelisation seven orders of magnitude coarser than a
dedicated narrowband search, and it is complementary to those surveys
rather than a deeper version of them.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/eirp_context_b.pdf}
\caption{Where this survey sits in spectral resolution. Native channel
width against observing frequency for every searched window (filled:
Class~A, fine-channel; open: Class~B, coarse-channel), with the
comparison programmes as horizontal segments: \citet{Enriquez2017},
\citet{Margot2023}, \citet{Price2020} and \citet{Mason2024}. The
distinctive point of this survey is its frequency regime, not its depth:
the channel widths plotted span about seven orders of magnitude --- which
is what the $\Delta\log\Delta\nu_{\rm ch}\approx7$ annotation marks ---
so sensitivities are not comparable between programmes. The
corresponding EIRP-versus-distance comparison, which requires that
warning to be read correctly, is Fig.~\ref{fig:contextapp} in
Appendix~\ref{app:notation}.}
\label{fig:context}
\end{figure}

A non-detection must become a quantitative exclusion statement:
\citet{Wright2018} formalised the ``Cosmic Haystack'' search volume,
\citet{Margot2023} its conversion into an upper bound on the
transmitter-hosting fraction, and \citet{EarthDetectingEarth2025} asked
where Earth's technosignatures would be detectable. That bound is valid
only if the sample represents the underlying population, which
interest-selected samples do not. Most surveys select by proximity, spectral type, planets or Earth
Transit Zone membership; the ALMA-specific form, disc selection, skews
the sample young and dynamically active. We instead take every star
within 40\,pc lying in the usable field of at least one public ALMA
observation (\S\ref{sec:sample}). That sample is \emph{work-list
complete but epoch-incomplete}, and this paper uses no other completeness
formulation: every star on the frozen work list that proves genuinely
covered is searched, but not every execution block those stars hold is in
this release. Of \NCoveredCand{} catalogue crossmatches,
\NGenuinelyCovered{} correspond to genuine ALMA coverage and \NStars{}
were searched; of the \NProgenitorEBTotal{} public execution blocks they
hold, \LedInScope{} have been searched and \LedCatalogue{} form the
frozen science sample reported here. It makes no completeness claim for the
local population itself. That choice removes technosignature-specific
selection and leaves the archival astrophysical selection in place; the
\NCensus{} sample entries are
${\sim}1$ per cent of the catalogued population within 40\,pc (the
\NStars{} actually searched here are ${\sim}0.5$ per cent), and the
residual coverage selection is characterised as a completeness function
(\S\ref{sec:sample}, Table~\ref{tab:searchspace}).

\section{Sample selection}
\label{sec:sample}

The sample is \emph{work-list complete but epoch-incomplete} within
40\,pc (Table~\ref{tab:quantities}), which is a much weaker condition
than volume completeness in the first half and an explicit limitation in
the second. The execution-block accounting closes as
follows, and every number in this paper refers to one of these
populations.

\begin{enumerate}[topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt,parsep=0pt,leftmargin=1.4em]
\item \LedProgenitor{} public execution blocks lie in the parent member
  observing-unit sets of the sample stars. This is the population in
  scope, and it splits in two.
\item \LedInScope{} of them have been downloaded, calibrated and
  searched; \LedNotProcessed{} have not. Every one of the
  \LedNotProcessed{} is repeat coverage: each sits in an observing-unit
  set from which another block was already taken, and the de-duplication
  rule below keeps one block per star and tuning. \LedNoCalib{} of them
  the archive offers as raw visibilities with no pipeline calibration at
  all. That is the first identity,
  $\LedInScope+\LedNotProcessed=\LedProgenitor$.
\item \LedProcessed{} blocks were processed in total,
  \TotalDataTB\,TB of raw data through one frozen pipeline. This is a
  \emph{different} population from the \LedInScope: it is those plus
  \LedNoProgLink{} whose member-OUS progenitor link the archive does not
  expose, so they cannot be placed in the ledger above. That is the second
  identity, $\LedInScope+\LedNoProgLink=\LedProcessed$. The two counts
  must not be added to each other.
\item Of the \LedProcessed, \LedCatalogue{} form the \emph{frozen science
  sample} reported here, \LedHoldout{} are the reserved calibration
  hold-out of \S\ref{sec:heldout}, and \LedNoWindow{} left no surviving
  window after the quality cut.
\end{enumerate}

We therefore make no claim to have searched the whole ALMA archive, only
every star on the work list. Table~\ref{tab:partitions} gives the
provenance of every dataset used anywhere in this paper.

 The procedure admits every star within 40\,pc of the Sun (Gaia~DR3
parallaxes, distances direct inversions $d = 1/\pi$; every entry $\pi \ge
25$\,mas with a parallax measured to better than \PlxAdqlPct{} per cent, which
is the condition the archive query applied) whose proper-motion-corrected
position at the observation epoch falls in the quoted field of view of at
least one public ALMA execution block. The star need not sit at the pointing centre,
and \S\ref{sec:exclusions} shows the quoted field of view admitted
\NNeverObsStars{} stars no observation in fact contains, so ``covered'' is a
property of our selection and not a guarantee. Disc, planet and
spectral type enter as descriptive metadata only.
Units are defined in \S\ref{sec:units}. This gives \NCensus{} unique target
stars, processed nearest-first. Because
trigger thresholds scale with $d^{2}$ at fixed noise, that order is
sensitivity-ranked, so the prevalence statements of \S\ref{sec:discussion} are
conditional on it.

Composition follows from the archival selection and is tabulated against the
reference census in Table~\ref{tab:selfunc}. The sample is F- and G-enriched
and deficient in M~dwarfs, and \ConcThreeWin{} of the \NWindows{} windows come
from \ConcThreeSys{} systems; \S\ref{sec:bias} gives the figures and their cost. One catalogue entry needs flagging here rather than only in the appendix,
because the released catalogue and the text would otherwise appear to
disagree: the entry ``$\gamma$~Lupi'' resolves by parallax to HD~139664 at
17.40\,pc, not to the naked-eye star, and is relabelled accordingly. No
measured value changes. Appendix~\ref{app:exclusions} lists the other
cross-match and exclusion edge cases in the same way, and any reader
comparing the released catalogue against this text should read it. The spectral-class proxy is \texttt{teff\_gspphot},
available for \NTeffSample{} of \NStars{} sample stars and missing preferentially for the
coolest objects, so the M fraction is a lower limit and the earlier-type
ratios upper limits. The debris-disc hosts contribute dusty continua, kept
from masquerading as anomalies by the continuum lane's screen
(\S\ref{sec:method}).

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/selection_bias.pdf}
\caption{The archival selection, which no limit in this paper escapes.
Grey: the spectral-class composition of the Gaia~DR3 objects within
40\,pc that carry a temperature. Blue: the same for the searched sample.
Counts are printed above each bar. The sample is enriched in F and G
stars by factors of four and three and depleted in M~dwarfs by a factor
two, so \NMSearched{} of the \NMCensus{} classified M~dwarfs within
40\,pc carry a searched window. Because M~dwarfs host most of the
habitable-zone planets in the solar neighbourhood, this is the single
most important limitation on what the null result means
(\S\ref{sec:bias}).}
\label{fig:selbias}
\end{figure}

