``Usable public ALMA coverage'' is a five-criterion decision tree, frozen
absent an erratum.

No star or window was excluded by an unlisted criterion, and none for short
integration.

\begin{figure*} \centering
\includegraphics[width=0.96\textwidth]{figures/selection_funnel.pdf}
\caption{Left: the selection funnel. The three denominators the text keeps
apart are the catalogued population, the \NCensus-entry ALMA-covered work
list, the ${\sim}\NGenuinelyCovered$ stars a public field genuinely contains,
and the \NStars{} searched. Right: the experiment in \NFunnelStepsWord{}
steps with the number surviving each, matching \S\ref{sec:summary} line
for line --- from the
public execution blocks in scope, through the pre-registered hold-out and
the window inventory, to the candidate tests. The
\NCtrl-control comparison at step~7 is a candidate-generation screen and
not a test of the null; step~8 is the same screen with the radial gradient
removed. Every count is read from the generated macros, so the figure, the
list and the released catalogue cannot disagree.}
\label{fig:funnel}
\end{figure*}

Because the rest of the paper is organised around that figure, its
right-hand panel is worth reading now rather than at
\S\ref{sec:summary}, where the same \NFunnelStepsWord{} steps are given
with every number attached. In brief, and in order: the
\LedProgenitor{} public execution blocks in scope are reduced to
\LedProcessed{} with a usable calibration and \LedCatalogue{} in the
science sample once the pre-registered hold-out is withheld; those yield
\NWindows{} spectral windows, of which \NWinA{} are fine-channel enough
to resolve drift; \NHits{} windows carry a threshold crossing at the
stellar position; \NStageOneWin{} of those also outrank all \NCtrl{}
spatial controls and \LocAllFlagLocal{} still do after the radial
correction of \S\ref{sec:radial}; \NStageLocalised{} survive the
visibility-domain test; \NStageOneUnattrib{} have no astrophysical
attribution; and \NCandidates{} are independently confirmed. The first
five steps assemble the data and its inventory and the remaining six test
it; the boundary is the threshold crossing, and everything from it onward
is the candidate sequence of \S\ref{sec:statistic}.

\begin{figure*}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.99\textwidth]{figures/coverage_waterfall.pdf}
\caption{Where this survey searched. \emph{Lower panel:} each searched
window is a horizontal segment on its host system's row, rows ordered by
distance and labelled alternately on the left and right axes; heavy blue
marks the fine-channel carrier search (Class~A) and light grey the
coarse-channel spectral-excess search (Class~B). \emph{Upper panel:} the number
of systems covering each gigahertz of sky frequency. The panel shows
coverage and search class only; the threshold distribution is
Fig.~\ref{fig:context}. The coverage is narrow,
clustered and repeatedly revisited: \UnionIntervals{} islands
concentrated near 230 and 345\,GHz, with summed window bandwidth
$\GrossOverUnion\times$ the union. The union is the frequency coverage; the
summed bandwidth is the integrated search effort, and the two answer different
questions (Scope of the constraints, \S\ref{sec:method}).}
\label{fig:waterfall}
\end{figure*}

