\section{Data and methodology}

Three abbreviations recur below. QA2 is ALMA's second-stage quality assurance, the stage that delivers calibrated data; a member observing
unit set (MOUS) is the scheduling container holding one or more execution
blocks; TDM and FDM are the correlator's time- and frequency-division
modes, giving coarse and fine channelisation respectively. Symbols,
including $T_\star$, $T_{\rm trig}$ and the power scales, are defined in
Appendix~\ref{app:notation}.
\label{sec:method}

The design is one shared archive-ingestion stage feeding two independent
search lanes, and Table~\ref{tab:algorithm} gives the data path of the primary
lane in order. For each target we mine, within a bounded budget, the
calibrated archival execution blocks covering the star's Gaia-propagated
position. Their visibilities feed (i) the \emph{primary} lane, a
Doppler-drift-corrected spectral-carrier search following \citet{Mason2024}
and \citet{Enriquez2017}, which converts each non-detection into an equivalent
isotropic radiated power limit
$\mathrm{EIRP_{\rm trig}} = 4\pi d^{2} S_{\rm min}\Delta\nu$. The product
$S_{\rm min}\Delta\nu$ is an integrated power only if the channel's effective
noise bandwidth equals its separation, which for a smoothed correlator it does
not; that is precisely what the response factor $C_{\rm response}$ corrects,
and we calibrate the correction against injected signals. Unresolved tones
of known amplitude were injected into real visibilities and recovered
through the unmodified pipeline. The measured factor is
$\times\HanMeasMed$ at the median, spanning
$\times\HanMeasCentred$--$\times\HanMeasEdge$ across correlator modes,
against the $\times\HanFacMed$ predicted analytically for the Hanning
response (Appendix~\ref{app:inject}): the two medians agree to one per
cent. The spread is window-to-window variation in the correlator response
and not an error on the estimator, so
$P_{\rm eff}=S_{\rm min}\Delta\nu\,C_{\rm response}C_{\rm smear}$
recovers the injected power. EIRPs are quoted to two significant figures, which is the precision
the flux scale supports. Here $S_{\rm min}$ is the primary-beam-corrected trigger-level flux density
defined below, $\Delta\nu$ the native channel width and $d$ the distance
(worked numerically in Appendix~\ref{app:conventions}). Five terms enter the systematic budget, each
multiplying $S_{\rm min}$ and hence EIRP$_{\rm trig}$ directly.

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=1.9em,itemsep=1pt,topsep=3pt,parsep=0pt]
\item The ALMA absolute flux scale: \BudFlux{} per cent band-weighted,
up to \BudFluxWorst{} per cent at Bands~9 and~10, and epoch-dependent.
\item Residual atmospheric phase after water-vapour correction and phase
referencing: a coherent point-source loss $\exp(-\sigma_\phi^{2}/2)$,
$\BudDecorLo$--$\BudDecorHi$ per cent at 230--345\,GHz. It is common to
star and annulus within the isoplanatic patch and so no threat to
exchangeability, but it is \emph{not} common to the injected tones that
calibrate $P_{90}$, which is why \S\ref{sec:excludes} carries it as a
one-sided bias.
\item The primary-beam model at the stellar offset: negligible on axis,
\BudPoint{} per cent at the worst pointing error.
\item Visibility calibration: bounded empirically at
\VisCalPct{} per cent. If the delivered amplitude scale were inconsistent
between execution blocks, the ratio of each window's measured
per-integration rms to its radiometric prediction would scatter from block
to block beyond what thermal noise explains. Over \VisCalWin{} windows in
\VisCalBlocks{} blocks the per-block median of that ratio is
\VisCalMed{} with a robust scatter of \VisCalPct{} per cent, against
\VisCalWithinPct{} per cent within a block --- so the term is real, is the
larger of the two, and is smaller than the flux-scale term. Including this term raises the combined budget from \BudCombOld{} to
\BudCombNew{} per cent, and \BudCombNew{} per cent is the figure quoted
throughout.
\item The instrumental spectral response: median $\times\HanFacMed$, the
largest of the five by an order of magnitude, carried explicitly as
$P_{\rm eff}$ by the reading rule below.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\emph{Gaia} distance errors are negligible,
\BudDist{} per cent typically. The same visibilities feed (ii) an
\emph{ancillary} line-excluded continuum lane
(Appendix~\ref{app:continuum}). In most windows an Hz-wide carrier is
unresolved within a 15.625\,MHz channel, so the data cannot show a one-channel
excess to be intrinsically narrow. Two survey classes are reported,
distinguished by the dimensionless drift parameter
\begin{equation}
\eta_{\rm drift} \;=\; |\dot\nu_{\max}|\,\tau_{\rm track}\,/\,\Delta\nu_{\rm ch},
\label{eq:etadrift}
\end{equation}
the channel-width fraction the \emph{maximum} searched drift
$|\dot\nu_{\max}|$ traverses over the full on-source track $\tau_{\rm track}$
at native channel width $\Delta\nu_{\rm ch}$. The track duration is written
$\tau_{\rm track}$ throughout, leaving $T$ for the search statistic alone. The track is the right time-scale, the de-drift step summing along it; $\eta_{\rm smear}$ (Appendix~\ref{app:conventions}) is the same ratio
over one integration. A window is \emph{drift-resolving} when
$\eta_{\rm drift}\gtrsim1$, carrying a carrier across enough
channels for its motion to be measured. The drift
ceiling inside $\eta_{\rm drift}$ is a search-grid boundary, the widest
trial drift the stack evaluates, not a prior on transmitter
drift rates; carriers drifting faster are missed, and
\S\ref{sec:detectable} gives the resulting selection function
(Fig.~\ref{fig:accel}).

\emph{Class~A} is the fine-channel spectral-carrier search: the
\NWinA{} windows of fine channelisation, native widths
\ChanAMinKHz--\ChanAMaxKHz\,kHz (median \ChanAMedKHz\,kHz;
\ChanAModePct{} per cent at the \ChanAModeKHz-kHz mode), spanning
$\eta_{\rm drift}=\EtaAmin$--$\EtaAmax$ (median \EtaAmed). It is the
only class with measured end-to-end recovery of a drifting carrier.
\emph{Class~B} is the coarse-channel spectral-excess search: the \NWinB{}
coarse windows, \PctCoarse{} per cent of the sample, native widths
\ChanBMinMHz--\ChanBMaxMHz\,MHz (\ChanBModeN{} windows at the
\ChanBModeMHz-MHz mode), spanning
$\eta_{\rm drift}=\EtaBmin$--$\EtaBmax$ (median \EtaBmed). It is
sensitive to persistent near-stationary excess power, carries no drift
discrimination, and is never described as a narrowband search anywhere
in this paper.

$\eta_{\rm drift}$ is the physical criterion and the classes are
labelled by the instrument. Channelisation is bimodal, with no window
between \ChanAMaxKHz\,kHz and \ChanBMinMHz\,MHz, an empty gap of factor
\ChanGapFac, so any boundary inside the gap assigns identical membership
and figures label the classes by name. The two criteria do not coincide
exactly, and the overlap is quantified:
\EtaBgeOneNow{} long-integration Class~B windows reach
$\eta_{\rm drift}\geq1$, and only marginally (maximum \EtaBgeOneMax),
where a one-to-two channel traverse supports no discriminative drift fit
after baseline removal; \EtaAltOneNow{} short Class~A windows fall below
unity and are conservatively kept in Class~A. On the physical criterion
alone \NEtaResolved{} windows of \NWindows{} reach $\eta_{\rm drift}\geq1$,
against \NWinA{} in Class~A, so the labels are channelisation labels and
``drift-resolved'' is used of the class only where the two agree. Each window's
$\eta_{\rm drift}$, $\eta_{\rm smear}$, trial drift count and maximum
searched acceleration $a_{\max}=\AccelCeilMed$--$\AccelCeilMax$\,m\,s$^{-2}$ are
columns of the released catalogue, so any reader may reclassify on the
physical criterion alone. Results are given per class first and combined
only where statistically appropriate (\S\ref{sec:technosearch}).

Both lanes exclude catalogued molecular transitions at tolerances suited to
their statistics. The continuum lane masks the common species at native
resolution before integrating; the carrier lane applies a
$\pm\MaskVWidth$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ stellar-frame velocity exclusion mask
(\S\ref{sec:linecost}) to any threshold crossing. \textbf{That half-width was set after the $\beta$~Pictoris crossings had
been inspected, and is the one element of the analysis not fixed in
advance.} We state it here, where the mask is introduced, rather than in a
later section, and give the check that resolves it in the same place.

Two results bear on whether the post-hoc choice matters. First,
reclassifying all \NHits{} crossings at $\pm20$, $\pm30$, $\pm50$ and
$\pm100$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ admits no new unattributed event at any width, and
CP$-$72~2713 remains the only unmasked one throughout; this is a one-sided
test and is reported as such (\S\ref{sec:linecost}). Second, and more to the point, the choice of half-width is not doing any
work, and Table~\ref{tab:maskrobust} shows why. The mask is applied to the
offset between a crossing and its nearest catalogued transition in the
observed sky frame, without the barycentric and systemic corrections, which
is why its half-width is generous: $\pm\MaskVWidth$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ has to
absorb those terms rather than assume them. Measured in that frame the
\MrNAtt{} line-attributed stage-1 windows all lie within
\MrAttMax\,km\,s$^{-1}$ and the \MrNUn{} unattributed ones all lie beyond
\MrUnMin\,km\,s$^{-1}$. The two populations are separated by a factor of
\MrGapRatio{} in velocity offset, so \emph{every} half-width between them
reproduces the released classification exactly, and the adopted value sits
inside that gap with a factor of \MrHeadLo{} of headroom on one side and
\MrHeadHi{} on the other. A Keplerian bound on the half-width, $\lesssim
13$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ where these molecules survive beyond 10\,au, is a
\emph{stellar-frame} velocity and is not directly comparable with a
number applied in the observed frame; we quote it as the physical scale
that motivates masking at all, not as an alternative that was tested. \begin{table}
\centering
\footnotesize
\caption{\textbf{Robustness of the stage-1 dispositions to the line-mask
half-width.} Offsets are measured in the observed sky frame, which is the
frame the mask is applied in (see text); they are not stellar-frame
velocities. ``line-attrib.'' counts the \MrNAtt{} stage-1 windows the
released catalogue attributes to molecular emission, ``unattrib.'' the
\MrNUn{} it does not. The adopted half-width is in bold. Any value between
\MrAttMax{} and \MrUnMin\,km\,s$^{-1}$ gives the released classification.}
\label{tab:maskrobust}
\input{tab_maskrobust_v395}
\end{table}

\emph{The mask is a classification mask, not a detection mask, and the
distinction matters.} It is applied after the search, to decide how an
event that has already been found should be interpreted; it is not applied
to the data before searching and it removes nothing from the detection
stage. No threshold moves, because masking changes the frequency space a
stage-1 event can be \emph{attributed} within while leaving the
per-channel noise untouched, and every masked window is searched exactly as
an unmasked one is. What the mask does define is the frequency space
\emph{excluded from the exclusion statement}: the survey covers
\UnionGHz\,GHz of unique sky frequency in \UnionIntervals{} clustered
islands (Fig.~\ref{fig:waterfall}), the effective default quoted
throughout is the ${\approx}\SearchedUnionGHz$\,GHz surviving the mask,
and no constraint is placed on carriers lying inside the excluded velocity
regions --- not because they could not have been detected, but because a
detection there could not have been distinguished from the molecular
emission the mask marks. All flux densities and EIRP
thresholds are total intensity (Stokes~$I$), summed over the two
parallel-hand cross-correlations with QA2 weights; no cross-hand
extraction or circularity test was performed, so the construction is
polarisation-blind in amplitude and a strongly polarised artificial
signal responds like an unpolarised one at fixed total power. The estimator is
safe in this respect, though the data have not been audited for it.
Both parallel hands are delivered for \NEbBothHands{} of the
\NEbPolKnown{} searched execution blocks whose polarisation states the
archive lets us resolve (\PctEbPolKnown{} per cent of the \NEB), so the
discrimination described next is available from the existing data for most
of the sample and needs no new observation. What the retained
products do not record is per-correlation flagging inside QA2, so we cannot
say what fraction kept both hands unflagged all the way through; one that lost
a hand would be $\sqrt{2}$ less sensitive than its threshold implies, with
nothing here to catch it. What Stokes~$I$ costs is discrimination, not
sensitivity: an artificial coherent carrier is more likely to be strongly
polarised than the unpolarised line and dust emission that fills this archive,
so a polarisation measurement would separate the two for free.

The three sensitivity quantities of Table~\ref{tab:quantities} are
defined here in full.

The trigger power $P_{\rm trig}$ is five times the window's
per-channel rms at its native channel width, for a transmitter at the star's
position matching the searched morphology. It is the level at which the search
fires and what every reference to the trigger below means; it is an internal pipeline
quantity, not a sensitivity to compare with a transmitter, and not a
significance. The two numbers to quote are $P_{\rm eff}$ and $P_{90}$.

The effective unresolved-carrier threshold $P_{\rm eff}$ is
$P_{\rm trig}$ corrected for the instrument's spectral response. ALMA applies
online Hanning smoothing, which leaves only \HanWorst{} to \HanBest{} of a
sub-channel tone's power in the peak channel depending on where in the channel
it falls, median \HanMed{} (Fig.~\ref{fig:hanning},
Appendix~\ref{app:conventions}). Nominal
thresholds are therefore optimistic by $\times\HanFacBest$ to
$\times\HanFacWorst$, median $\times\HanFacMed$, and $P_{\rm
eff}=\HanFacMed\,P_{\rm trig}$ is the number to compare with any transmitter
power. It follows from the $0.25/0.5/0.25$ kernel and is window-specific
($\times\HanFacAvgLo$--$\HanFacAvgHi$ for the \NHanOther{} windows with online
channel averaging), and injection through the response kernel reproduces it to
\HanMeasDevPct{} per cent (median $\times\HanMeasMed$;
Appendix~\ref{app:inject}). It is a sensitivity
correction and not a completeness, it is not small, and we treat it as the
principal physical threshold.

The completeness limit $P_x$, the power at which measured
recovery reaches $x$ per cent, is a different quantity again:
$P_{50}=1.10\,P_{\rm trig}$ and $P_{90}>2\,P_{\rm trig}$ on the calibration
window's drifting class (Appendix~\ref{app:inject}). Transfer to other bands,
channelisations and integration times is measured on
\StratWindows{} further configurations, where $P_{50}$ spans
$\StratTransferLo$--$\StratTransferHi\times$ its median
(Appendix~\ref{app:inject}); $P_{95}$ remains \emph{not determined}. What this
survey has is a measured reference recovery function whose transferability is
now bounded by measurement, and
not a measured Class~A completeness function.

Every headline EIRP is quoted beside its native channel width, and the
catalogue carries $P_{\rm trig}$ and both response-corrected values per
window.

\medskip \noindent Three signal morphologies are detectable here, and
\S\ref{sec:detectable} quantifies each against real windows: a continuous
frequency-stationary carrier, a continuously drifting one, and an intermittent
one.

\begin{figure*}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.94\textwidth]{figures/hanning_response.pdf}
\caption{Why the nominal channel threshold is not the transmitter
threshold. ALMA applies online Hanning smoothing by default, so an
intrinsically unresolved carrier is spread over neighbouring channels and
the peak channel holds only part of its power: half at a channel centre,
\HanWorst{} on a boundary (first three panels; blue is the peak channel,
the dashed line the carrier). The search threshold is set in units of the
channel noise, so the power a transmitter must radiate to reach it is
larger than the nominal figure by the reciprocal of that fraction
(right-hand panel), from $\times\HanFacBest$ to $\times\HanFacWorst$
with a survey median of $\times\HanFacMed$. This is the whole of the
difference between $P_{\rm trig}$ and $P_{\rm eff}$ in
Table~\ref{tab:quantities}.}
\label{fig:hanning}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Final candidate definition and statistical interpretation}
\label{sec:statistic}

The spatial screen was frozen before the candidate list was examined, in the form that ranks the stellar
position against \NCtrl{} single-position controls under one global noise
normalisation. Validation showed that form to be non-exchangeable: the
stellar rank is displaced from the uniform expectation, and the mechanism
is understood. Images are corrected for the primary-beam response, so the
true noise rises with distance from the pointing centre, while the search
divides every position by a single scale estimated over the whole window.
The control probes therefore sit at systematically different effective
noise from the star, which is at the phase centre.

A radius-corrected normalisation removes that radial component by
construction, standardising each probe within its own radius bin with no
free parameter (Appendix~\ref{app:provenance}). Tested prospectively on \RadValStarN{} windows that played no part in
building it, the correction does not restore exchangeability (\S\ref{sec:radval}). The
corrected stellar rank remains inconsistent with uniformity out of sample,
and its median moves \RadValStarToward{} the exchangeable value rather
than toward it. A correction that does not deliver a calibrated statistic
cannot be preferred merely because its mechanism is understood.

The originally frozen screen therefore remains the primary
candidate-generation statistic --- the statistic that decides which
windows are examined --- and neither form is treated as a significance
test. The word ``primary'' is used in that sense throughout. It is not to
be confused with the separate question of which normalisation gives the
preferred comparison for the false-alarm \emph{rate}, which
\S\ref{sec:technosearch} treats on its own and where both forms are
reported side by side. Because that distinction is easy to lose while
skimming, we fix a notation for it here and keep it for the rest of the
paper: a tail quantity computed against the screen's own
exchangeability-violating null is written $\Pscr$, never $p$. It is the
probability of the observed count under a stated and imperfect null, not
a calibrated significance. The symbol $p$ is reserved for genuine
significance levels, such as the Kolmogorov--Smirnov tests on rank
distributions. Both are reported for every window in the released
catalogue, the corrected one as a robustness comparison, and the
consequences for the candidate list are given in full in
Appendix~\ref{app:provenance}. What disposes of an event is not its rank
under either normalisation but the physical evidence: whether the emission
is localised at the stellar position in the visibilities
(\S\ref{sec:vistest}), whether it coincides with a catalogued transition
in the stellar frame, and whether it recurs in an independent epoch.

Neither form is a significance test. Ranking the star
against \NCtrl{} control positions drawn from the same field is a
convenient and powerful way to decide which windows deserve examination.
It is not a $p$-value: $1/(\NCtrl+1)=\RankFloorVal$ is the
\emph{resolution} of the ensemble, the smallest rank it can express, and
not the probability that noise produced the window.

Two consequences follow immediately and both are structural. First, no
single window in this design can carry survey-level significance by rank
alone: against a survey-wide Bonferroni scale
%
\begin{equation}
\alpha_{\rm survey}=\frac{0.05}{\NWindows}=\BonfScaleCalc,
\qquad
\frac{\RankFloorVal}{\alpha_{\rm survey}}=\BonfRankRatio,
\label{eq:bonf}
\end{equation}
%
the ensemble's floor is \BonfRankRatio{} times too coarse
(Appendix~\ref{app:falsealarm}). Second, a technosignature claim here
therefore requires independent recurrence or independent astrophysical
confirmation, and never rank alone.

The decisive test is interferometric, not statistical. A carrier
at the stellar position must appear coherently in the real part of the
visibilities after phase rotation to that position, with an imaginary part
consistent with zero. That is a physical statement about where the
emission is, it does not rest on the exchangeability assumption the
spatial screen violates, and it is therefore the test on which every
disposition in this paper turns. The screen that ranks the star against
\NCtrl{} control positions decides only which windows are examined.

The selection logic is five steps, in order. The first two generate
candidates; the third is the primary physical test.

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=1.9em,itemsep=2pt,topsep=3pt,parsep=0pt]
\item Threshold crossing. A channel$\times$drift cell at the
stellar position reaches $T_\star\geq T_{\rm trig}$. A fixed trigger
power, not a calibrated $p$-value.
\item Spatial prioritisation. The stellar statistic exceeds all
\NCtrl{} control positions in the same window. A candidate-generation
mechanism: it decides which windows are examined and carries no
significance of its own, for the reasons above.
\item Visibility-domain localisation. The emission is at the
stellar position in the visibilities. Each event's own channel is followed
along its recovered drift rate, the local continuum is removed per
integration, and the visibilities are phase-rotated to the star: emission
centred there gives a positive real part with an imaginary part consistent
with zero (\S\ref{sec:vistest}). This is the first test in the chain to ask a physical question about the
sky, where the two before it ask a comparative question about an ensemble;
it is also the first a window can fail on evidence, not on rank.
\item Astrophysical identification. The event is checked against
catalogued molecular transitions in the stellar frame and against
instrumental signatures.
\item Independent recurrence. It repeats at the same tuning in an
\emph{independent epoch}, or acquires independent confirmation.
\end{enumerate}

The visibility test was applied to the \VxNTestedWin{} windows the
screen flagged and not to all \VxNCross{} threshold crossings, and the
reason is an operational one worth stating exactly. The pipeline deletes
each measurement set once its search completes and retains only the
derived products, so the test could be run only while a block was in
flight, which in practice meant on windows the screen had just promoted.
Checking the surviving data directly, \VxNEbHeld{} execution blocks still
hold visibilities and neither contains a threshold crossing: no crossing
window can now be tested from data in hand. Extending the test to the
remaining \VxNUntestedWin{} crossing windows therefore means
re-downloading and recalibrating \VxNBlocks{} execution blocks,
\VxTB\,TB, which this pipeline's measured cost model puts at about
\VxHours\,h of processing. The frequencies and drift rates needed to
place each fit are recoverable from the retained search products, so the
work is re-calibration rather than a re-search, and it is specified and
costed rather than merely intended; it is not done here, and until it is,
the statement below applies to \VxNTestedWin{} windows and not to
\VxNCross. The asymmetry is one-sided: the windows tested in
the visibility domain are those the image-plane screen selected, so the
test as applied here vets the screen's output rather than replacing the
screen. Stage~(iii) does not use the control ensemble at all, which is why
it is worth its cost. It runs on recalibrated raw data, it can fail a window the
image-plane statistic promotes, and it carries an internal positive
control: the same fit recovers the $\beta$~Pictoris CO emission at the
stellar position in \NFitAttRecov{} of its \NFitBpicWin{} windows at
$\FitAttReLo$--$\FitAttReHi\sigma$. Every stage-1 event is carried
through all five stages in Table~\ref{tab:stages}. Nothing short of (v) is called a candidate.

All delivered channels of each spectral window were searched: no
edge-channel or bandpass-roll-off trimming was applied before extraction,
so residual bandpass structure near a window edge is carried entirely in
the calibration-residual term of the systematic budget rather than removed
by flagging. The consequence is checked directly --- the distance of every
threshold crossing from its window edges is a released catalogue quantity,
and \S\ref{sec:technosearch} reports it for the flagged windows.

The primary statistic is formed on extracted spectra, never on images or
visibilities, from the QA2-calibrated measurement set as delivered, per
integration and per native channel, with the archive's flagging carried
through untouched and no regridding
(Table~\ref{tab:algorithm}, step~3). What the extraction returns at a
propagated sky position $\mathbf{x}$ is the real part of the weighted vector
average of the phase-shifted visibilities, which is the natural-weighted
dirty-map value there. There is no deconvolution, so a quoted $S_{\rm peak}$
in a field with extended emission is a dirty-map amplitude. Baseline removal
follows extraction (step~4). The primary-beam correction enters the quoted
thresholds and fluxes (\S\ref{sec:frames}) and leaves untouched the extracted
amplitudes the statistic ranks. For a sky position $\mathbf{x}$, each per-integration
spectrum $I(\mathbf{x},t,\nu)$ is baseline-subtracted by a block median along the
\emph{frequency} axis. No temporal median is applied at any stage
(\S\ref{sec:ancillary}). The residual dynamic spectrum is stacked along each
trial linear drift rate $\dot\nu$ with
inverse-variance weights, and the statistic is the maximum of that
stack over the channel$\times$drift grid,
%
\begin{equation}
T(\mathbf{x})=\max_{\dot\nu,\;\nu}\;
\frac{\sum_{t} I\!\left(\mathbf{x},t,\nu+\dot\nu t\right)\big/\sigma^{2}(t,\nu)}
     {\left[\sum_{t}1\big/\sigma^{2}(t,\nu)\right]^{1/2}},
\label{eq:tstar}
\end{equation}
%
a matched filter whose denominator is the noise of the stacked spectrum.
$T(\mathbf{x})$ is neither assumed nor shown to be Gaussian or $\chi$
distributed under the null, which is why $T_{\rm trig}$ here denotes a trigger level and not a tail probability; Appendix~\ref{app:falsealarm} quantifies the
departure. Only when $\sigma$ is the same for every integration and channel does it
reduce to a single-channel amplitude divided by one noise scale; otherwise the
weights vary along the track. The scale
$\sigma(t,\nu)=1.4826\,\mathrm{MAD}_{i=1\ldots\NCtrl}\!\left[I_i(t,\nu)\right]$,
that is $1.4826\times$ the median absolute deviation taken
\emph{across the control positions} at each integration $t$ and
each channel $\nu$, which makes it global in position, local in frequency and
in time, and shared by the star and by every control.

The across-position median
enters only in forming that deviation and is never subtracted from the
numerator, so $T$ is an absolute stacked signal-to-noise carrying a
cross-position noise scale, and it measures no local contrast.

We use this form rather than a region maximum because $T$ is evaluated at one position, identically at the star and at each control, and that is what makes
the rank interpretable. If instead the stellar side takes a maximum over
$n_{\rm src}$ positions while the controls remain single positions, the two
sides are no longer exchangeable: under the null the region maximum exceeds
all $N_{\rm ctrl}$ controls with probability $n_{\rm src}/(n_{\rm src}+N_{\rm
ctrl})$, not $1/(N_{\rm ctrl}+1)$. With $n_{\rm src}=\StatNsrc$ and $N_{\rm
ctrl}=\NCtrl$ that is \StatRegFloorPct{} per cent rather than 0.2, a floor
under which most crossings flag whatever the data contain. The argument is
combinatorial and refers to no window: it holds for any statistic, any noise
distribution and any field. A region-maximum form of the statistic fails for exactly this reason;
\S\ref{sec:aumic} gives the comparison and Table~\ref{tab:bothstats} audits
every window either form flags. A line filling
the primary beam therefore drives star and annulus up together instead
of cancelling.

\begin{table*}
\centering
\scriptsize
\caption{The statistical data path, in order. Steps 1--8 run
identically at the stellar position and all \NCtrl{} controls; only
step 9 onward distinguishes them.}
\label{tab:algorithm}
\begin{tabular}{@{}rl@{}}
\hline
1 & calibrated measurement set from the ALMA archive \\
2 & stellar position propagated to the observation epoch (Gaia DR3) \\
3 & spectral extraction at that position, native channelisation \\
4 & per-integration baseline removal: block median along frequency ($W=\MedWin$) \\
5 & stack along each trial linear drift rate \\
6 & robust noise map $\sigma(t,\nu)$: MAD across the \NCtrl{} controls \\
7 & $T(\mathbf{x})$ from Eq.~\ref{eq:tstar}, an inverse-variance-weighted stack \\
8 & repeat 3--7 at \NCtrl{} control positions on a ring \\
9 & threshold: $T_\star\geq5$ (a crossing) \\
10 & rank test: $T_\star>\max_i T_i$ (stage-1 flagged window) \\
11 & molecular-line mask, $\pm50$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ stellar frame \\
12 & vetting: recurrence and astrophysical attribution \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

The searched drift-rate range is the larger of (a) a generic
frequency-scaled default (12--13\,Hz\,s$^{-1}$\,GHz$^{-1}$), the
\citet{Mason2024} fiducial with a threefold safety margin, and
(b) for confirmed exoplanet hosts, the maximum line-of-sight orbital
acceleration of the innermost known planet, so a transmitter on a
short-period planet cannot drift out of the window. Drift rate maps
onto line-of-sight acceleration through the first-order Doppler
relation
%
\begin{equation}
a_{\rm los} \;=\; c\,\frac{\dot\nu}{\nu},
\label{eq:drift}
\end{equation}
%
so the ceiling is quoted in cross-band-comparable form $\dot\nu/\nu$:
12--13\,Hz\,s$^{-1}$\,GHz$^{-1}$ is $(1.2$--$1.3)\times10^{-8}$\,s$^{-1}$,
that is $|a_{\rm los}| = 3.6$--$3.9$\,m\,s$^{-2}$ independent of observing
frequency, the acceleration at ${\sim}0.04$\,au around a solar-type star.
Earth-motion contributions lie two orders of magnitude below and are subsumed
by the grid. The grid is uniform in $\dot\nu$ over $n_{\rm drift}$ steps
(2--1944 across windows, recorded per window), so a carrier midway between
trial rates leaves a residual drift traversing at most \DriftGridFineHalf{} of
a native channel over the longest fine-class track, and the resulting
amplitude loss is an order below the recovery slope measured between $T_\star=5$ and $10$. Two limits of the model matter more. The rates searched are
\emph{apparent} topocentric ones, so the survey does not separate a
deliberately chirped carrier from an accelerating platform and constrains only
linear apparent drift inside the ceiling; and an emitter need not be tied to a
known planet at all, a free-flying platform or rotating structure accelerating
as it chooses and falling outside these constraints without being excluded by
them. For circular orbits the ceiling covers a transmitter co-orbiting an
Earth analogue by three orders of magnitude and Proxima~b comfortably, while
TRAPPIST-1\,b ($a_{\rm los}=\OrbAccTrapb$\,m\,s$^{-2}$) sits at it and c
($\OrbAccTrapc$\,m\,s$^{-2}$) reaches it at modest eccentricity, which raises
the periastron value by up to $(1{+}e)/(1{-}e)^{2}$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:accel}).
That value is the maximum over orbital phase, not a typical one. TRAPPIST-1
transits, so its inclination is measured and effectively edge-on, and the
relevant figure is the edge-on one: TRAPPIST-1\,b exceeds the ceiling over
\TrapPhaseLo--\TrapPhaseHi{} per cent of its orbital phase, depending on which
end of the \DriftCeilLo--\DriftCeilHi\,Hz\,s$^{-1}$\,GHz$^{-1}$ ceiling
applies, and is searchable through the rest of it.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/drift_acceleration.pdf}
\caption{What the drift search does and does not reach, as a physical
selection function. \emph{Upper:} the drift ceiling of each of the
\NWinA{} Class~A windows (points), converted to the line-of-sight
acceleration it corresponds to, $|a| = c\,\dot\nu/\nu$, against observing
frequency; shaded above is unsearched. The boundary is flat because the
grid was built at constant acceleration, \AccelCeilMed\,m\,s$^{-2}$, and
not at constant drift rate. \emph{Lower:} the same boundary in the units
the search works in, where it is not flat: the same
\AccelCeilMed\,m\,s$^{-2}$ is \AccelDriftLo\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at
\AccelFreqLo\,GHz and \AccelDriftHi\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at
\AccelFreqHi\,GHz. Reference accelerations, all frequency-independent and
so horizontal in the upper panel: Earth's rotation
(\AccelEarthRot\,m\,s$^{-2}$ at the equator) and orbit
(\AccelEarthOrb\,m\,s$^{-2}$), which a terrestrial transmitter carries
whether or not it intends to; the most demanding known planet of any
target, \AccelWorstName{} (\AccelWorstVal\,m\,s$^{-2}$); and the next most
demanding, \AccelSecondName{} (\AccelSecondVal\,m\,s$^{-2}$).
\AccelWorstName{} is the one case the grid does not contain, and it misses
by \AccelShortPct{} per cent (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).}
\label{fig:accel}
\end{figure}

Across the \NWindows{} windows maximum searched drift rates span
\DriftKHzLo--\DriftKHzHi\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ and trial drift rates per window
2--\BpSixTrials{} (median \TrialsMed), the largest being the narrow, finely
channelised $\beta$~Pictoris Band~6 window (\S\ref{sec:bpicval}).
\NWinNarrow{} windows are narrower than 100\,MHz.
The threefold margin matters. At $1\times$ the search would have
been blind to two of its threshold-crossing windows, including the AU~Mic
crossing, while at $5\times$ no disposition changes; transmitters drifting
faster than the \DriftCeilLo--\DriftCeilHi\,Hz\,s$^{-1}$\,GHz$^{-1}$ ceiling
remain unconstrained.
Intra-integration smearing is negligible for \NSmearOk{} of the
\NSmearWin{} windows that carry a value: at most 1.10 channels, median
0.001. \NSmearLo{} windows have $\eta_{\rm
smear} < 0.99$ (Appendix~\ref{app:conventions}); \NSmearWorst{} of them, all \SmearWorstChanKHz\,kHz Band~6 windows and so
among the most drift-capable here, carry effective thresholds
\SmearWorstPenLo--\SmearWorstPenHi$\times$ nominal, and \NSmearFlagged{} are
stage-1 flagged windows. Channel widths are
effective resolutions from the measurement sets' spectral
window tables (Appendix~\ref{app:conventions}).



\subsection{Ancillary and validation analyses}
\label{sec:ancillary}

Nothing here generates candidates; it is validation, and the detail is in
the appendices. Two demonstrations carry the weight. Synthetic tones of
known amplitude were injected into real visibilities and recovered through
the unmodified pipeline --- \NTrials{} trials across six window
configurations, three bands and both classes
(Appendix~\ref{app:inject}) --- which establishes that the machinery
recovers a drifting narrowband carrier, the searched morphology. And
$\beta$~Pictoris CO is recovered and localised in two transitions and
\NStageOneBpicEb{} blocks (\S\ref{sec:bpicval}), which establishes that
it finds a real feature at a known stellar position. The baseline step is
a per-integration median along \emph{frequency}
(Table~\ref{tab:algorithm}, step~4) and so cannot remove a one- or
two-channel feature. Appendix~\ref{app:provenance} reports the three
quantified checks that bound undetected pipeline faults.

\subsection{The experiment in summary} \label{sec:summary}

Everything above is the experiment in detail. Here it is in
\NFunnelStepsWord{} steps, with the number surviving each one. The
right-hand panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:funnel} is this list, drawn, and
Table~\ref{tab:stages} carries every stage-1 event through the last
four.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.35em,itemsep=1pt,topsep=3pt,parsep=0pt]
\item \LedProgenitor{} execution blocks. Every public ALMA
execution block whose field contains a catalogued star within 40\,pc.
\item \LedProcessed{} processed. Those delivered with a usable
calibration, all put through one frozen pipeline with no per-target tuning.
The \LedNotProcessed{} not processed are repeat coverage of a block already
in hand.
\item \LedCatalogue{} in the science sample. \LedHoldout{} blocks
are withheld by a rule fixed before any of them was looked at --- a hash of
the block identifier --- and are never used to set a threshold, only to
check one. \LedNoWindow{} yielded no usable window.
\item \NWindows{} spectral windows. Each window of each retained
block, de-duplicated by identifier.
\item \NWinA{} Class~A windows. Those whose channels are fine
enough to resolve a drifting carrier. This is the primary experiment; the
remaining \NWinB{} support only a search for unresolved spectral excess.
\item \NHits{} threshold crossings. Windows in which the
matched-filter statistic at the stellar position reaches
$T_\star\geq5$ somewhere on the drift grid.
\item \NStageOneWin{} stage-1 windows. Of those, the ones in which
the star also outranks all \NCtrl{} spatial control positions in the same
window. This is a prioritisation, not a test: the control ensemble is not
exactly exchangeable with the star, and \S\ref{sec:heldout} measures by how
much.
\item \LocAllFlagLocal{} survive radius correction. Standardising
each control probe within its own radius bin removes the one gradient we
can demonstrate in the control ensemble. \LocAllLost{} windows lose the flag
and \LocAllGained{} gains it; all are marginal, and no disposition changes.
\item \NStageLocalised{} localised at the star by the
drift-following visibility fit, which recovers $\beta$~Pictoris where it
should.
\item \NStageOneUnattrib{} without an astrophysical attribution.
\NStageOneLine{} of the \NStageOneWin{} are carbon monoxide, \NStageOneBpic{}
of them the $\beta$~Pictoris debris disc. Chance alone predicts
\PriMean{} unattributed events at this stage against
\NStageOneUnattrib{} observed under the primary frozen screen
($\Pscr\approx\RobP$) and \PriNUnattrib{} under the radius-corrected
normalisation ($\Pscr\approx\PriP$). Neither is a calibrated significance,
for the reason \S\ref{sec:radval} gives, and neither disposes of an
event (Table~\ref{tab:falsealarm}).
\item \NCandidates{} independently confirmed candidates. Each of
the
\NStageOneUnattrib{} has at least one further execution block of the same
star covering its frequency, \NUnattRepeatBlocks{} in all, and the feature
recurs in none of them: the largest stellar statistic anywhere in those repeats is
$T_\star=\UnattRepeatMaxT$ and \NUnattRepeatAboveCtrl{} exceed their own
controls. The separations are \UnattSepMinD--\UnattSepMaxD\,d, so this
tests persistence over days and not over years. A drift-following,
continuum-subtracted fit to the visibilities --- the estimator that
recovers $\beta$~Pictoris in \NFitAttRecov{} windows at
$\FitAttReLo$--$\FitAttReHi\sigma$ --- finds nothing at the stellar
position in any of the four, the largest $|{\rm Re}/\sigma|$ being
\FitUnattMaxAbs. Nothing is independently
confirmed.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent A reader who wants to check any single line can: the survivor
count at every step is a column filter on the released catalogue, and
\texttt{reproduce\_from\_catalogue\_v385.py} performs exactly those filters.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

The \NWindows{} windows are the size of the processing exercise, not of the
technosignature search. The primary experiment is the fine-channel search:
\NWinA{} Class~A windows toward \NSysClassA{} systems, in which a drifting
narrowband carrier can be resolved and its recovery has been measured by
injection. The \NWinB{} Class~B windows, \PctCoarse{} per cent of the
total, are a complementary search for channel-confined excess power; their
channels are 7.8--31.25\,MHz wide and in most of them drift cannot be
discriminated at all. Results are reported per class, and the combined
count appears only where it describes the archive processed rather than the
sensitivity achieved.

\begin{figure*}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{figures/classa_sensitivity.pdf}
\caption{\emph{(a)}~Class~A sensitivity. Cumulative number of
independent systems whose best window reaches a given EIRP, for
$P_{90}^{\rm sel}$, the power passing the complete automated selection
(solid), and for $P_{90}$, which reaches the trigger only (dashed). The
shaded band is the systematic budget: the window-to-window transfer
bracket $\times\StratTransferNineLo$--$\times\StratTransferNineHi$, which
dominates, together with the one-sided $+\BudDecorHi$ per cent
atmospheric-decorrelation bias, which can only make the quoted power
optimistic. It is asymmetric for that reason. \emph{(b)}~The temporal
selection function: systems grouped by the longest separation between
their searched epochs, an independent epoch being defined as a separation
of more than \EpSep{} day. \textbf{For the \EpOne{} systems with a single
block and the \EpSameDay{} whose blocks all fall within one day --- left
of the dashed line --- independent confirmation was not possible at all},
whatever the sensitivity in panel~(a). Recurrence over years can be tested
for \EpYears{} systems.}
\label{fig:classasens}
\end{figure*}

The choice of detection statistic makes the non-detection more conservative,
not less: the symmetric form of Eq.~\ref{eq:tstar} flags a strict subset of
what the region-maximum form flags, \NSpatial{} windows against
\NRegionMaxFlag, with none flagged by the symmetric form alone
(\S\ref{sec:aumic}, Table~\ref{tab:bothstats}).



Per-target values, including each band's searched frequency range, are in the
released catalogue (Data Availability).

\subsection{Barnard's Star and Wolf~359}
\label{sec:barnardswolf}

Barnard's Star and Wolf~359 are the second- and fourth-nearest stellar
systems. We find no prior mm or submm technosignature limit for
either, so these appear to be the first. Both are non-detections in public Band~6 archival
data. The nominal EIRP$_{\rm trig}$ trigger thresholds are
$\EirpBarnLo$--$\EirpBarnHi$\,W for Barnard's Star over \NWinBarn{}
windows and $\EirpWolfLo$--$\EirpWolfHi$\,W for Wolf~359 over
\NWinWolf, with continuum upper limits of 0.575 and 0.453\,mJy.

\subsection{Technosignature search}

Two ranking conventions are carried in parallel throughout this section,
and it is worth saying at the outset which is which. The originally frozen
screen ranks the stellar position against \NCtrl{} controls under a single
global noise normalisation; a radius-corrected variant standardises the
probes within radius bins. Neither is exchangeable, so neither is a
significance test. We treat the frozen screen as primary for a procedural
reason rather than a statistical one --- it was fixed before the candidate
list was examined, whereas the correction was not, and the correction
fails a prospective test of the property it was built to repair
(\S\ref{sec:radval}). \textbf{The choice does not affect the
conclusion.} Both conventions yield zero confirmed candidates. They differ
only in the count of unattributed, non-recurring events ---
\NStageOneUnattrib{} under the frozen screen and \PriNUnattrib{} under
the radius-corrected one --- and no event is promoted to a candidate under
either. Both are released for every window in the catalogue.
\label{sec:technosearch}

All \NStarBands{} star/band datasets yielded at least one carrier-lane result,
\NWindows{} spectral-window measurements after the catalogue audit removed
\NDup{} duplicate rows, Bands~6 and~7 dominating
(Table~\ref{tab:perband}). Nominal trigger
thresholds span $\EirpMin$--$\EirpMax$\,W at a median of $\EirpMedian$\,W
(Figs.~\ref{fig:waterfall} and \ref{fig:context}), the deepest being reached
toward the nearest systems, $1.6\times10^{13}$\,W for the UV~Ceti pair at
2.7\,pc, though depth also tracks integration time and channelisation.

The statistics come strictly before the astrophysics, in three named
stages that do not promote one another. A \emph{crossing} is one
channel$\times$drift cell at the stellar position reaching $T\geq5$:
\NHits{} of the \NWindows{} windows contain one, and \PctCrossClassA{} per
cent of them are Class~A. That is a trials effect rather than a physical one: a
median Class~A window holds $\CellsMedA$ channel$\times$drift cells against
\CellsMedB{} for a median Class~B window, a factor \CellsRatioAB, so a cell at the trigger is correspondingly rarer in the coarse class. A
\emph{stage-1 flagged window} is a window whose stellar statistic
also exceeds all \NCtrl{} controls: \NStageOneWin{} do, in
\NStageOneSys{} systems and \NStageOnePairs{} star--band pairs. That
screening device selects a window for examination
(\S\ref{sec:statistic}). Each of them is then
carried past that floor by a local scramble test ((v) below), which asks
whether a feature exceeds the noise at its own position without discriminating
spatially; in \LNNCtrlClear{} of the \LNNWindows{} windows the control-ring
maximum clears it too, so candidacy still turns on the first-stage
star-versus-control comparison. A \emph{confirmed technosignature} is a stage-1 flagged window that survives
every stage of \S\ref{sec:statistic} and then recurs or is independently
confirmed. There are none.

Three terms carry this section, in the sense fixed by
Table~\ref{tab:quantities}: a \emph{crossing} is a channel$\times$drift
cell at the star reaching $T_\star\geq5$; a \emph{stage-1 flagged window} is a
window whose $T_\star$ also exceeds all \NCtrl{} controls, which prioritises
it and establishes nothing; a \emph{candidate} survives every stage of
\S\ref{sec:statistic} and recurs or is independently confirmed.

The outliers fall into two groups.
\NStageOneLine{} of the \NStageOneWin{} sit within
$\pm\MaskVWidth$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ of a molecular transition in the star's own
frame and carry independent CO evidence: \NStageOneBpic{} are
$\beta$~Pictoris, in \NStageOneBpicThree{} Band~3 and \NStageOneBpicSix{}
Band~6 windows drawn from \NStageOneBpicEb{} execution blocks, at
$\BpicDvLo$ to $\BpicDvHi$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ and $T_\star$ from \BpicTsymMin{}
to \BpicTsymMax; one is the published foreground emission at HD~48370. That
is \NStageOneSources{} astrophysical sources, not \NStageOneWin{} events,
and the recurrence is the point: a frozen pipeline recovers the same disc in
two transitions and \NStageOneBpicEb{} independent blocks without being told
it is there.

The remaining \NStageOneUnattrib{} have no astrophysical attribution.
How many should chance produce? One formulation answers that question and
the rest are pipeline diagnostics, so only the first is used here.
\emph{The primary false-alarm formulation of this paper is the
trigger-conditioned, block-resampled expectation}: it requires the
position both to rank first among the control ensemble and to reach the
trigger, which is what a stage-1 event actually is, and it resamples by
execution block rather than by window, because windows within a block
share weather, calibration and correlator setup. Every other expectation
quoted anywhere in this paper conditions on rank alone and therefore
predicts a different quantity; they are collected, labelled and compared in
Table~\ref{tab:falsealarm}, which has been placed in
Appendix~\ref{app:falsealarm} so that the main line of argument carries one
number rather than seven.

Five of these expectations answer the wrong question. An expectation built on the rate at which a no-signal position ranks first among the control ensemble
--- whether that rate is the exchangeable $1/(\NCtrl+1)$, the measured
pseudo-star rate, or either of those with the tail excess folded in ---
predicts the number of windows in which the star would \emph{rank first}.
A stage-1 event needs more than that: the position must also reach the
trigger. Comparing a rank-first expectation with a count of stage-1 events
is comparing two different quantities, and it is the source of the
apparent disagreement between 3.1, 3.2, 3.9, 4.4 and 4.7.

The correctly conditioned null is measurable from the stored control
vectors. In a given window a control probe reproduces a stage-1 event
only if it both reaches $T_{\rm trig}=\StageNullTrig$ and exceeds every
other probe; exactly one probe per window is the maximum, so the
probability that the stellar position would have done so, treated as
exchangeable with the probes, is $1/(\NCtrl+1)$ in those windows whose
control maximum reaches the trigger and zero in the rest. That condition
is restrictive and informative: only \StageNullEligible{} of the
\StageNullAllWin{} windows with a stored ensemble have a control maximum
that reaches the trigger at all, \StageNullEligiblePct{} per cent, and
\StageNullEligibleA{} of those are Class~A. \emph{This is why every
stage-1 event in the survey is Class~A}, and it also means the choice of
reference class, which earlier versions of this analysis treated as an
open question, largely dissolves once the trigger condition is imposed.

Resampling execution blocks with replacement, and applying the measured
non-exchangeability of the stellar position as a factor
$\times\PriTail$, gives the expectation the observed count should be read
against. Probabilities are quoted to one significant figure throughout:
the count is small and the tail factor that normalises the rate is itself
known only to $\PriTailLo$--$\PriTailHi$, so further digits would be
spurious. That uncertainty is carried in the quoted range rather than
added afterwards as a caveat.

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.3em,itemsep=1pt,topsep=3pt,parsep=0pt]
\item Primary, radius-corrected statistic: \PriMean{} expected
over the \PriNWin{} windows with stored control ensembles, of which
\PriNElig{} can reach the trigger from noise at all; 95 per cent interval
\PriQLo--\PriQHi{} against \PriNUnattrib{} observed, giving
$\Pscr\approx\PriP$ (range $\PriPLo$--$\PriPHi$ across the tail-factor
uncertainty).
\item Robustness, originally frozen screen: the same expectation
against \RobNUnattrib{} observed, giving $\Pscr\approx\RobP$.
\end{itemize}

\noindent The statement to carry forward is therefore that
\PriNUnattrib{} unattributed events were observed against
\PriMean{} expected under the calibrated, block-clustered null, $\Pscr\approx\PriP$, which is consistent with chance. The frozen screen's
$\Pscr\approx\RobP$ on \RobNUnattrib{} events is the reason the radial
normalisation was examined in the first place, and \S\ref{sec:radial}
shows that the apparent excess is an artefact of that screen's
position-dependent rank bias rather than a property of the sky. Neither
number is evidence about any individual event.

An excess this size needs only a small unmodelled tail in the control ensemble, and
\S\ref{sec:radial} demonstrates that one exists. That is not a free
explanation. The same noise model sets the recovery fractions of
\S\ref{sec:dwell}, so a tail large enough to remove the excess would also
mean the quoted completeness is optimistic, by roughly the same factor,
since both scale with the rate at which the ensemble produces an extreme
value. The two cannot be traded independently, and we have applied such a
correction to neither.


The four hosts are not a quiet sample, and it would be
convenient but wrong to leave that unsaid. \HostNActive{} of the
\HostNTot{} carry activity indicators in SIMBAD
(Table~\ref{tab:hosts}, queried \HostQueried) and \HostNMult{} is
catalogued as multiple; CP$-$72~2713 in particular is a K7Ve star flagged
as an emission-line, rotationally variable X-ray source, which is the
profile of a star that flares. Magnetically active stars do produce
coherent bursts at centimetre wavelengths, and although extrapolating that
to the millimetre is not straightforward, we cannot exclude a
stellar-activity origin for these events on the evidence assembled here.
What can be said is that activity does not account for them either: an
active star would still be a compact source \emph{at the stellar
position}, and the visibility test finds no such source for any of the
four. Activity is therefore a live alternative to instrumental noise, not
a live alternative to the disposition.

\begin{table}
\centering
\scriptsize
\caption{\textbf{Activity and multiplicity status of the \HostNTot{} hosts
of unattributed stage-1 events}, from SIMBAD object-type lists queried
\HostQueried. Distances are inverted parallaxes. Activity indicators are
X-ray, emission-line, flare and rotational-variability classifications;
``none catalogued'' means SIMBAD carries no such flag, not that the star is
known to be inactive.}
\label{tab:hosts}
\input{tab_hosts_v395}
\end{table}

All four have a persistence test, all four fail it, and the test
is short. Each has at least one further execution block of the same star
whose tuning covers the event's frequency: \NUnattRepeatLo{} to
\NUnattRepeatHi{} of them, \NUnattRepeatBlocks{} in total
(Table~\ref{tab:unattributed}). The feature recurs in none. But a count of
repeats is not a baseline, and the baselines here are days rather than
years: of the \NUnattRepeatBlocks{} repeats, \NUnattSepResolved{} are
separated from their event by a resolvable interval and that interval runs
\UnattSepMinD--\UnattSepMaxD\,d, while the remaining
\NUnattSepSameDay{} fall on the same day or within the same scheduling
block. \emph{So what these tests exclude is a transmitter persistent over
hours to days.} A source with a duty cycle measured in months or years
would not be caught by them, and nothing here bounds one. Across all \NUnattRepeatBlocks{} repeat
windows the largest stellar statistic is $T_\star=\UnattRepeatMaxT$,
\NUnattRepeatCross{} cross the threshold at all,
\NUnattRepeatAboveCtrl{} exceed their own window's control maximum and
\NUnattRepeatFlag{} are stage-1 flagged windows. Repeat coverage is matched on the window rather than on the crossing
frequency, which the release records only where the pipeline stored a
peak. CP$-$72~2713 is the case examined in detail
(\S\ref{sec:cp72}) because its repeat block is the deepest and closest
in configuration.

\subsubsection{The visibility-domain localisation test}
\label{sec:vistest}

\emph{Every stage-1 window has been tested in the visibility domain, twice,
and none of the unattributed ones behaves like emission at the star.}
The image-plane rank is a screen; the physical question
is whether the emission sits at the stellar coordinates, and the
visibilities answer it directly. Phase-rotating every visibility to the
star turns emission centred there into a constant real offset, so its real
part measures the source and its imaginary part must be consistent with
zero; emission elsewhere in the field is suppressed in the real part and in
general leaves a non-zero imaginary part. The test therefore separates
\emph{at the star} from \emph{displaced from the star}; it does not by
itself separate unresolved from resolved, because a centrally symmetric
resolved source is also centred on the star.

We recalibrated all \NVisTested{} stage-1 execution blocks from the raw
archive data --- \NVisUnatt{} unattributed and \NVisAtt{} attributed to
carbon monoxide --- and applied the test at the recorded peak frequency of
each event, with eight control positions on the annulus the image-plane
test uses (Table~\ref{tab:visibility}).

The attributed events are the test's controls, and they are why the null
result on the other four can be believed. \NVisAttRecov{} of the
\NVisAtt{} return emission at the stellar position at
$\VisAttReLo$--$\VisAttReHi\sigma$, every one of them above its own
controls: the $\beta$~Pictoris CO belt is recovered in the visibilities in
both transitions and in seven separate execution blocks. Of the
\NVisAttMiss{} that do not, one is the strongest possible negative ---
HD~48370, whose real part is large ($\VisHdFortyRe\sigma$) but whose
imaginary part is $\VisHdFortyIm\sigma$ from zero, exactly what emission
displaced from the phase centre produces, and independently known to be a
foreground cloud resolved several beams away. The other is a
$\beta$~Pictoris Band~3 block for which no measured peak frequency
survives in the frozen release and the test had to be run at the frequency
measured in its sibling blocks; it returns nothing, and we report it as a
limitation of the substituted frequency rather than as a failure of the
line.

Against that, the \NVisUnatt{} unattributed events, panel by panel in
Fig.~\ref{fig:unattribvis}: on this test \NVisPointSource{} of them shows
emission at the star. The largest real
part is $\VisMaxReSig\sigma$; 61~Vir does not exceed its own controls; and
HD~14055 returns a $\VisMaxReSig\sigma$ real part with a
$\VisHdImSig\sigma$ imaginary part. A source centred on the star would
leave the imaginary part at zero, so at face value that excess is
displaced; but a phase-calibration residual or a small astrometric error
produces the same signature, the uncertainties here are internal and assume
independent visibilities, and \NVisTested{} events give \NVisTested{}
looks. The defensible statement is that at this significance the uniform
test does not localise the excess, not that it has localised it away from
the star.

That comparison needs a caveat, because the test is not equally
sensitive to the two groups. It uses a single channel and no continuum
subtraction, identically for every event, which is what makes the thirteen
comparable. A broad line fills that channel completely, so the test is
fully sensitive to the attributed CO events. A narrowband carrier that
drifts occupies the channel for only part of the track, and the tracks
here sweep \VisDilUnattLo--\VisDilUnattHi{} channels for the unattributed
events, so a uniform single-channel test can lose such a carrier by as
much as $\times\VisDilUnattFacLo$--$\times\VisDilUnattFacHi$ in
signal-to-noise.

\emph{So we ran the sensitive estimator on all \NFitTested{} events as
well.} For each event, and for each integration, we take the channel the
carrier occupies at that moment following its recovered drift rate,
subtract a local continuum estimated from reference channels either side
of the track in the same integration, and phase-rotate to the star. Eight
positions on the control annulus and four off-event frequencies in the same
window give the comparison. Table~\ref{tab:visfit} gives the result and it
is decisive in both directions.

The estimator works: $\beta$~Pictoris is recovered at the stellar position
in \NFitAttRecov{} of its \NFitBpicWin{} windows at
$\FitAttReLo$--$\FitAttReHi\sigma$, and HD~48370 comes out with a large
real part, $\FitHdFortyRe\sigma$, but an imaginary part
$\FitHdFortyIm\sigma$ from zero --- the displaced-source signature its
foreground identification predicts, and the reason it is not counted as a
recovery at the star. \NFitAttUnreachable{} window
cannot be measured this way: its drift track sweeps more channels than the
continuum reference leaves, and we report that as a limitation of the
estimator rather than as a null.

And the four unattributed events show nothing. The largest
$|{\rm Re}/\sigma|$ among them is \FitUnattMaxAbs, \NFitUnattAtStar{} are
consistent with a source at the star, and no control position or control
frequency anywhere in the thirteen exceeds $\FitCtrlMax\sigma$.

One caution about the continuum applies to CP$-$72~2713 in particular.
That window's stellar continuum is \VisContMJy\,mJy at
$\VisStarSnr\sigma$, comparable to any narrowband excess it could carry,
so a fit that does not remove the continuum along the drift track will
report the continuum instead. The fit used here removes it per
integration, and returns $\FitCpRe\sigma$ at the stellar position with an
imaginary part of $\FitCpIm\sigma$. A simpler treatment of the same
visibilities, which leaves the continuum in, returns $\VisCpFit\sigma$;
the difference is the continuum, not the carrier.



