\subsubsection{Which normalisation is authoritative}
\label{sec:radval}

\noindent\fbox{\parbox{0.955\columnwidth}{\footnotesize
\textbf{The authoritative normalisation is the originally frozen screen.}
Every number in this paper's abstract, results and conclusions is computed
under it. The radius-corrected alternative was built, tested prospectively
and \emph{not} adopted, because it failed the validation set for it
(Appendix~\ref{app:radius}). Where the two are compared, the comparison is
labelled as such.}}

\medskip

\noindent The choice does not change the confirmed-candidate count, which
is zero under either form. The two differ only in the count of
unattributed, non-recurring events: \NStageOneUnattrib{} under the frozen
screen and \PriNUnattrib{} under the radius-corrected normalisation.
Neither form is exchangeable, so neither assigns those events a calibrated
false-alarm probability, and the disposition of every one of them rests on
the visibility-domain test and on recurrence rather than on either rank.
That is the sense in which the spatial statistic is used here: it is a
\emph{prioritisation diagnostic} that decides which windows are examined
further, and not a detection statistic that decides which windows are real.
The evidence for the displacement, the radial mechanism, the pseudo-star
control and the prospective test that returned \RadValVerdict{} are set out
in Appendix~\ref{app:radius}.

\subsubsection{Survey-level false-alarm calibration}

The per-window rank probability, its survey-level counterpart, the
pseudo-star and stratum tests that show the control ensemble behaves as an
ensemble, and the trials budget in cells are all calibration rather than
result, and are collected in Appendix~\ref{app:falsealarm}. What the main line of argument needs from them
is the two statements above: the tail rate is measured, and no window in this
survey approaches the survey-wide correction.

\subsubsection{Dispositions of the remaining outlier windows}

The third stage-1 flagged window, towards HD~48370 in Band~6, is the largest excess here in
absolute terms and the smallest in relative ones, $T_\star=\HdTsym$ against
a control-ring maximum of \HdRingMax, a margin of \HdMargin. Star and ring rising together is what emission
filling the primary beam looks like. The velocities agree immediately:
\citet{Cataldi2023} report prominent CO($2{\to}1$) towards HD~48370 at $v_{\rm
bary}\approx41$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ and flag it as possible foreground cloud
contamination, and our own frame chain returns $v_{\rm
bary}=+41.2$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ for this crossing, so we recover their line and
confirm it kinematically. Three checks close the circumstellar alternative.
The same authors report the disc itself undetected in CO and in carbon. The
sight line runs through the outer Galaxy, where a flat rotation curve reaches
the measured velocity only at a kinematic distance of \KinDistKpc\,kpc, which
nothing local could produce. And the block's $^{13}$CO($2{\to}1$) window has a
control maximum of \HdThirteenRingMax{} against \HdThirteenStar{} at the star,
so the field carries bright $^{13}$CO that the star does not, resolved
\HdCoOffsetBeams{} synthesised beams away. Appendix~\ref{app:provenance} gives
the Band~8 [C\,\textsc{i}] tuning check, which says the same thing by
omission. The rank statistic identifies nothing here, a stated limitation of
the spatial test.

\subsubsection{Line exclusion: definition and cost}
\label{sec:linecost}

The exclusion regions are defined before any crossing is classified, and
those frequencies are \emph{excluded from the experiment} rather than
vetoed within it: the experiment declines to classify them instead of
declaring whatever is found in them astrophysical. Each catalogued transition of
\MaskNSpecNew{} species is masked to $\pm\MaskVWidth$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ in
the stellar frame; Appendix~\ref{app:linemask} gives the kinematic
justification for that half-width, the species criterion, and the
counter-argument that a communicator might deliberately choose a natural
reference frequency; Appendix~\ref{app:linecostfull} gives the cost
species by species.

The search statistic is computed inside the masked channels;
the mask is applied afterwards, at the reporting stage. That wording
matters, because ``excluded from the experiment'' could otherwise be read
as meaning those channels were never examined. They were: the threshold
and rank tests run over every delivered channel, and the mask then removes
the masked intervals from the \emph{reported} search domain, because a
crossing coincident with a catalogued transition cannot be dispositioned
as artificial on this data alone.

That ordering makes a completed robustness check possible, and we report
it here rather than leaving it to an appendix, because it answers the most
obvious objection to the mask. Removing the mask entirely adds no
candidate. Of the \NHits{} threshold crossings, \MaskCrossIn{} fall
inside a tube: \MaskCrossInStageOne{} are stage-1 flagged windows already
dispositioned as CO in Table~\ref{tab:flagged}, and the remaining
\MaskCrossInNoFlag{} do not exceed their own control ensembles. So the
mask costs \MaskUnionPct{} per cent of the unique-frequency union and
nothing else; had it been absent, the candidate list would be identical.
The caveat that survives is one of principle rather than of outcome: a
transmitter deliberately parked on a natural reference frequency, which is
what \citet{CocconiMorrison1959} argued a communicator might choose,
would lie in unsearched reported space and this survey would not claim it
either way.

\subsection{What this experiment excludes, and what it does not}
\label{sec:excludes}\label{sec:pninetysel}

\noindent For Class~A the complete automated selection recovers a carrier
nine times in ten above $P_{90}^{\rm sel}=\PromoteLoA$--$\PromoteHiA$\,W
per window, median $\PromoteMedA$\,W; per system, taking each system's
best window, $\PromoteSysLoA$--$\PromoteSysHiA$\,W with a median of
$\PromoteSysMedA$\,W. The per-system figure is the one to quote.
Read every value as an upper bound on reach rather than a central
estimate: the injected tones that calibrate them suffer no atmospheric
decorrelation while a real carrier does, so they are optimistic by
$+\BudDecorLo$ to $+\BudDecorHi$ per cent and cannot err the other way.
Appendix~\ref{app:notation} defines the chain from $P_{\rm trig}$ to
$P_{90}^{\rm sel}$.

\emph{Excluded.} For a continuously transmitting, unresolved carrier
lying outside the molecular-line mask, within the searched frequency range
and inside the linear-drift grid, and present during one of the archival
epochs, the fine-channel search recovers the signal at least nine times in
ten above $P_{90}$. That threshold is $\PNinetyWinBestA$\,W of EIRP in the
deepest Class~A window and $\PNinetyWinMedA$\,W at the median one; it is a
property of the individual window, and the per-window value is a column of
the released catalogue.

$P_{90}$ is not the survey's sensitivity, and the difference matters. It is calibrated against the trigger, but a window does not select a
signal for technosignature vetting merely because the trigger fires: the
stellar statistic must also exceed every one of that window's \NCtrl{}
control positions, and those maxima sit above the trigger. Across Class~A
they run $\GateLoA$--$\GateHiA\sigma$ with a median of
$\GateMedA\sigma$, and only \NGateBelowTrig{} windows
(\PctGateBelowTrig{} per cent) have a gate below $T_{\rm trig}$.

The sensitivity of the experiment is therefore the power at which
a carrier passes the complete automated selection, which is each window's
$P_{90}$ scaled by its own gate:
$P_{90}^{\rm sel}=\PromoteLoA$--$\PromoteHiA$\,W, median
$\PromoteMedA$\,W, and per system, taking each system's best window,
$\PromoteSysLoA$--$\PromoteSysHiA$\,W with a median of
$\PromoteSysMedA$\,W over the \NPromoteSys{} Class~A systems. That is
$\times\PromoteFacMed$ the median $P_{90}$, and it is the number quoted in
the abstract, in Table~\ref{tab:searchspace} and in the conclusions. It
inherits the one-sided atmospheric-decorrelation bias of
$+\BudDecorLo$ to $+\BudDecorHi$ per cent that every injection-calibrated
power in this paper carries, so it is an upper bound on the survey's reach
and not a central estimate (Table~\ref{tab:p90budget}). $P_{90}$
remains in the catalogue and in the boxed rule above as the
matched-filter completeness, which is what a reader comparing filters
rather than surveys wants. Both are columns of the released file.
Figure~\ref{fig:dwellsel} shows the same completeness against duty cycle,
and Fig.~\ref{fig:classasens} the per-system spread and the epoch coverage
that limits confirmation. The campaign covers \StratWindows{} configurations rather than all
\NWinA. The limit is computational, not methodological: each injected
window is re-searched end to end through the frozen pipeline over the full
drift grid, so the campaign already costs a substantial fraction of the
survey's own search time, and extending it to every window would cost more
than the original reduction. That is why the window-to-window transfer is
reported as a measured bracket rather than removed, and why widening the
campaign is the recommendation of \S\ref{app:futurework} that would most
directly improve the headline number. The stratified campaign measures how
far the recovery curve itself moves between configurations: across the
\StratWindows{} injected windows $P_{90}$ ranges over
$\times\StratTransferNineLo$ to $\times\StratTransferNineHi$ of its
median-window value and $P_{50}$ over $\times\StratTransferLo$ to
$\times\StratTransferHi$, so a single quoted threshold should be read
with a factor of about two of spread around it, and the catalogue carries
each star's own bracket. No such carrier was found toward any of the
\NSystems{} systems searched.

The dominant term in the uncertainty is the window-to-window transfer of the injection calibration,
$\times\StratTransferNineLo$--$\times\StratTransferNineHi$, and on top of
it sits a one-sided atmospheric-decorrelation bias of $+\BudDecorLo$ to
$+\BudDecorHi$ per cent that can only make the figure optimistic; the
$\pm\BudCombNew$ per cent calibration budget on any one window's flux scale
is smaller than either. Table~\ref{tab:p90budget} gives the full
breakdown. It is quoted to two significant figures.
Table~\ref{tab:p90budget} is the budget. $P_{90}$ is linear in every factor
that builds it, and in $d^{2}$, so each entry is a percentage of the answer.
The independent errors combine to $\pm\BudCombNew$ per cent, dominated by
\BudDominant: ALMA's delivered absolute calibration is a few per cent at
the low bands and up to \BudFluxWorst{} per cent at Bands~9 and~10, and the
Class~A sample is weighted toward Bands~6 and~7. Parallax is negligible
even though it enters squared, \BudDist{} per cent typically and
\BudDistWorst{} per cent for the one close binary with a poor solution. The
injection campaign's own finite size contributes \BudMC{} per cent
(\BudBoot{} bootstrap resamples, 95 per cent interval
$\times\BudMCLo$--$\times\BudMCHi$), and residual pointing at
\BudPointArc\,arcsec contributes \BudPoint{} per cent, the star being at
the phase centre where the beam is flat.

One worked example makes the combination unambiguous. The median Class~A
window, \WorkedStar{} Band~\WorkedBand, has
$P_{90}=\WorkedPNinety\times10^{15}$\,W. The two-sided terms combine in
quadrature to $\pm\WorkedTwoSided$ per cent, giving
$\WorkedLoW$--$\WorkedHiW\times10^{15}$\,W. The one-sided terms then
apply to the upper end only, since they cannot make $P_{90}$ smaller,
taking it to $\WorkedBiasW\times10^{15}$\,W. And the window-to-window
transfer bracket, a different kind of quantity again, spans
$\WorkedTransLoW$--$\WorkedTransHiW\times10^{15}$\,W about the same
central value. The three are not added together.

Three further terms are listed separately, and they are not all of the
same kind. A carrier at an unknown position within a channel is attenuated
by a factor the analysis corrects at its median, leaving $\BudPhaseLo$ to
$+\BudPhaseHi$ per cent that no amount of calibration removes; that is an
unknown about the transmitter, not about the telescope. Atmospheric
decorrelation is a \emph{one-sided bias} of $+\BudDecorLo$ to
$+\BudDecorHi$ per cent: the injected tones that calibrate $P_{90}$ are
added to already-calibrated visibilities and suffer none of it, while a
real carrier does, so every $P_{90}$ in this paper is optimistic by that
amount and cannot be optimistic in the other direction. And the
window-to-window spread, $\BudTransLo$ to $+\BudTransHi$ per cent, is a
measured property of the survey for the \StratWindows{} windows the
campaign injected into --- but for the \NWinA{} Class~A windows it did not,
it is a genuine transfer uncertainty, and it is the largest term in the
budget by a factor of several. The practical consequence is that the second
significant figure of $P_{90}$ is meaningful for a single injected window
and not for the survey, and that the quoted values should be read as an
upper bound on the survey's reach rather than a central estimate.

\begin{table}
\centering
\caption{Systematic uncertainty budget on $P_{90}$. The first block are
independent errors on the quoted number and combine in quadrature; the
second are carried separately: the sub-channel phase is an unknown about
the transmitter, the decorrelation term is a \emph{one-sided bias} that
makes $P_{90}$ optimistic and cannot make it pessimistic, and the
window-to-window term is a transfer uncertainty for the windows the
campaign did not inject into. Folding them into one total would
double-count the per-window bracket the catalogue already carries.}
\label{tab:p90budget}
\footnotesize
\input{tab_p90budget_v385}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/dwell_selection.pdf}
\caption{The duty-cycle selection function, measured. Recovery
probability against transmitter power, in units of $P_{90}$, and duty
cycle $f_{\rm dwell}$, from \NDwellTrials{} injection trials. The abscissa
is in units of $P_{90}$, which is the pipeline's nominal trigger power multiplied by $\times\HanFacMed$ for the spectral response and
intra-integration smearing and then by $\times\PNinetyOverTrig$ from the
injection campaign, so a value of 1 here is
$\times\PNinetyInTrig$ the trigger power and not the trigger power itself.
The quoted $P_{90}$ thresholds apply along the top of this plot. Recovery falls away
below $f_{\rm dwell}\simeq0.3$ and a transmitter on a tenth of the time
needs several times $P_{90}$ to reach the same recovery, so the EIRP
limits in this paper constrain persistent and high-duty-cycle emission
and say progressively less about brief or scanning beacons. The dwell
injections are zero-drift, so this selection function is measured for a
stationary carrier and transferred to the drifting primary experiment; the
joint drifting-and-intermittent cell is the one cell of
Table~\ref{tab:classcomp} that remains unmeasured.}
\label{fig:dwellsel}
\end{figure}

\emph{Not excluded.} A transmitter absent during the archival observation,
or on for less than about a tenth of the time. Emission inside the
$\pm\MaskVWidth$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ molecular-line mask, at any strength.
Drift outside the searched grid, or drift curved enough to leave a channel
within the track. Signals broad enough to be removed with the continuum
baseline. Anything below the window's own completeness threshold. And any
emission from the \NGaiaCensus{} stars within 40\,pc that ALMA has not
observed, which is almost all of them.

Two blind spots are worth naming. The search
is Stokes~$I$ only, so it makes no use of polarisation: a fully polarised
carrier still appears in $I$ at its full strength and is not missed, but
the discrimination a polarised signal would offer against unpolarised
astrophysical emission is unavailable, and a per-hand search of the
\NEbBothHands{} blocks that deliver both parallel hands would gain up to
$\times\PolGain$ in signal-to-noise on such a carrier
(\S\ref{app:futurework}). And the frequency coverage is the archive's, not
a choice: the survey has no sensitivity at all to the ``magic frequency''
arguments that have guided targeted searches --- the 1420\,MHz line, its
multiples, the water hole, or $\pi$ and $e$ times them --- because none of
them falls in an ALMA band. A transmitter deliberately placed on such a
frequency is outside this experiment by construction, and its absence here
is not evidence against it.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

Everything in this section is conditional on what the archive chose to
observe, and two features of that choice bound every inference drawn
below. \PctDiscProposal{} per cent of the searched stars were observed for
debris-disc or planet-formation science, which skews the sample young,
dusty and dynamically active, and puts circumstellar CO --- the source of
\NStageOneLine{} of the \NStageOneWin{} stage-1 events --- in the field
of a large fraction of the windows. And \NMSearched{} of the \NMCensus{}
M~dwarfs within 40\,pc carry a searched window, so the most numerous
stellar class in the volume is the least represented here. Neither is a
defect of the search; both are properties of an archive assembled for
other purposes, and together they mean that the quantities below describe
\emph{these} stars and not the 40\,pc population.

\subsection{What signals could ALMA archival data actually detect?}
\label{sec:detectable}

Each morphology is illustrated with a real window, the completeness entries
of Table~\ref{tab:classcomp} and Fig.~\ref{fig:sens2d}. Denominators count the \NSystems{} systems whose deepest
threshold in the class lies at or below the case power.

\emph{(i) A continuous, frequency-stationary carrier} in a coarse channel is
the best case. The UV~Ceti pair at 2.7\,pc has a deepest window whose trigger
is $1.6\times10^{13}$\,W and whose effective threshold is
$\EirpEffDeepest$\,W, and a channel-confined carrier there is recovered at
near-ideal $\sqrt{N}$ gain (\S\ref{sec:dwell}). Read on the trigger,
\CaseDetCoarseNow{} systems of \NSystems{} reach the numerical EIRP of the
Arecibo planetary radar and \CaseDetCoarseTwoNow{} reach twice it; read on
the effective threshold, which is the comparison a transmitter power calls
for, \CaseDetEffArec{} do and \CaseDetEffArecTwo{} reach twice it. The survey's best case is therefore a few times that numerical EIRP.

\emph{(ii) A continuously drifting carrier} in a fine 488-kHz channel is the
only morphology with a measured end-to-end recovery curve
(Appendix~\ref{app:inject}): above ${\sim}8\times10^{13}$\,W nominal it is
recovered in \RecTwice{} per cent of trials with its drift inside the
searched ceiling, and \CaseDetFineNow{} systems reach that depth in
Class~A. The measured
$\StratTransferLo$--$\StratTransferHi\times$ transfer applies to the power
at which that recovery is reached, not to the recovery fraction itself.

\emph{(iii) An intermittent carrier, on for a quarter of the track,} above
${\sim}2\times10^{14}$\,W is detected toward \CaseDetDwellNow{} mid-distance
systems. Recovery falls rapidly at smaller dwell fractions
(Table~\ref{tab:dwell}): below $f_{\rm dwell}\sim0.1$ only the nearest windows
carry information.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/sensitivity_2d.pdf}
\caption{Two-dimensional sensitivity against window centre frequency for all
\NWindows{} searched windows, symbol by channelisation class, the axis along
which completeness differs (Table~\ref{tab:classcomp}). \emph{Left axis}: the
nominal trigger threshold $P_{\rm trig}$, an internal detection statistic.
\emph{Right axis}: the effective threshold at the \emph{median} response,
$P_{\rm eff}=\HanFacMed\,P_{\rm trig}$, which is the power to compare with a
transmitter (\S\ref{sec:method}); the released per-window ratio
$P_{\rm eff,total}/P_{\rm trig}$ runs $\PeffRatioLo$--$\PeffRatioHi$, so read a
single window's threshold from the catalogue and not from this axis. Red rings mark the \NSmearLo{} windows whose intra-integration smearing
correction exceeds 1 per cent. Grey lines mark the
Bands 3, 6 and 7 concentrations; dotted, the Arecibo \ArecGHz-GHz EIRP
benchmark, a power scale only. Note the two decades of scatter at fixed
frequency: there is no single survey sensitivity.}
\label{fig:sens2d}
\end{figure}

\subsection{What does the null result mean?}
\label{sec:null}

No persistent, independently confirmed unresolved spectral-carrier
technosignature has been found within the domain searched
(\S\ref{sec:technosearch}); an intermittent emitter is not excluded.
The EIRP$_{\rm trig}$ values are nominal trigger powers
(\S\ref{sec:method}), and completeness-calibrated exclusion holds only in
Class~A, where the measured recovery curve applies.

One exclusion deserves the converse emphasis. The line mask removes
$\pm\MaskVWidth$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ stellar-frame tubes around the
catalogued transitions, whose rest frequencies are the
`magic' channels \citet{CocconiMorrison1959} argued a communicator might
choose. This survey is blind by construction to a transmitter parked there in the
stellar frame, a false-positive control purchased with \MaskGrossPct{} per
cent of gross bandwidth and one physically motivated class of signal. A carrier deliberately placed on
CO($2{\to}1$) is not undetectable by ALMA, it is unsearched here, and those
channels are retained in the release for anyone bringing independent spatial
or time-domain discrimination. Of the \NExoPlanetsTab{} planets known around the
\NExoHostsTab{} hosts, only TRAPPIST-1\,b reaches the
drift ceiling in its edge-on worst case, on accelerations
$GM_\star/r^{2}$ from NASA Exoplanet Archive parameters maximised over phase
and inclination.
The ceiling is derived from line-of-sight Doppler acceleration of
plausible planetary hosts and does not model transmitter-side oscillator
drift; a transmitter whose own reference drifts faster than the kinematic
budget would fall outside the searched grid, and we make no claim about
that case. Figure~\ref{fig:accel} shows the ceiling as a selection
function of period and
host mass, the host-mass dependence being weak ($a\propto M_\star^{1/3}$ at
fixed period). Coverage eligibility and search completeness differ: \S\ref{sec:sample}
audits the \NProgenitorEBTotal{} public blocks in scope against the \NEB{}
in the headline sample. The remainder is accounted for and not
outstanding, and the split closes exactly:
$\LedProgenitor=\LedSciInScope+\LedHoldoutInScope+\LedNotProcessed$,
being the science-sample blocks that lie in the progenitor set, the
reserved calibration hold-out (\S\ref{sec:heldout}), and blocks never
searched because they are duplicate coverage of the same star and tuning
(\S\ref{sec:sample}), of which \LedNoCalib{} carry no pipeline
calibration in the archive at all. The \LedSciOutScope{} science blocks
whose member-OUS link the archive does not expose sit outside this
identity and are counted in \S\ref{sec:sample} instead.

Against \citet{Wright2018}'s haystack, only Band~3 falls inside that
axis's 10\,MHz--115\,GHz span (\UnionBandThree{} of 115\,GHz); the
remaining \UnionBandsFourEight\,GHz in Bands~4--8 is new territory. The
median threshold detects a $10^{13}$\,W transmitter only within
${\sim}0.9$\,pc. Against the sole prior ALMA-archival search
\citep[][28 bycatch targets at $\geq$1.01\,kpc]{Mason2024}, the \NSystems{}
systems here reach median per-system selection sensitivities
$P_{90}^{\rm sel}$ that are ${\sim}\MasonRatioSel\times$ lower in EIRP
($\MasonMedPsel$\,W; ${\sim}\MasonRatioNew\times$ if the weaker
trigger-level $P_{90}$ is used instead, which is closer to the definition
their own published threshold carries, so the quoted ratio is the
conservative one). That comparison is almost
entirely the distance term and not a statement about the instruments: the
ratio of $d^{2}$ alone is ${\sim}\MasonDsqRatio$, so in \emph{received
flux} the present survey is the shallower of the two. The EIRP ratio is the
right comparison for a transmitter power and the wrong one for
sensitivity, and we give both. We do not combine these fractions into one coverage number, because
the axes are strongly coupled and a product of marginals would misstate the
overlap by orders of magnitude.

The meaningful comparison with previous work is axis by axis rather than on a single figure of merit: this survey differs from
centimetre-wave searches in frequency, spectral resolution, number of
systems, EIRP completeness, drift range and temporal coverage
simultaneously, and Fig.~\ref{fig:context} makes the central point, which
is that its novelty is the frequency regime and not depth. For readers who
want the conventional Continuous Waveform Transmitter Figure of Merit it
is computed in Appendix~\ref{app:cwtfm}, together with the reason the
survey-union convention flatters every archival survey and why we prefer
the per-system value. On that metric this survey is about four orders of
magnitude less competitive than major centimetre-wave programmes, which is
the correct conclusion and not a criticism of either.

How far that null reaches is set by recurrence, not by the rank statistic. No
single event in this design can clear the survey-wide threshold
(Appendix~\ref{app:falsealarm}), so a genuine but weak transmitter seen in one epoch
is not separable from the \PseudoExpFlags{} chance events the survey expects,
which is exactly the position CP$-$72~2713 leaves us in. What separates them is a second epoch or an independent astrophysical
identification, and here the distinction between a repeat block and a
temporally independent epoch matters. Of the \EpTot{} systems,
\EpOne{} were searched in a single execution block and \EpMulti{} in
more than one --- but \EpSameDay{} of those \EpMulti{} have all their
blocks within \EpSep{} day of each other, which is one observing session
and not an independent test of persistence. \textbf{Only \EpIndep{}
systems, \EpIndepPct{} per cent, have epochs separated by more than
\EpSep{} day}: \EpDays{} over days, \EpMonths{} over months and
\EpYears{} over years. Recurrence over years can therefore be tested for
fewer than a third of the sample. A further
\NSysRecArchOnly{} hold archived blocks that were not both processed,
\NSysMultiEB{} in all, so the recurrence test could in principle be extended
to \PctSysRecurrenceArch{} per cent of the sample with no new observing time.

In plain terms, ALMA has observed \NStars{} nearby stars as a by-product of
other science, across parts of the millimetre band no technosignature search
had examined, and we found no channel-confined carrier the surrounding sky
could not also produce. On the few gigahertz, few hours and single morphology
class each star happened to be observed with, that says little about how
common transmitters are.

\subsection{Why no single window in this design can be significant}
\label{sec:floor}

One structural limitation deserves to be stated in its own right rather
than left in the methods, because it constrains what any survey built this
way can claim. The spatial screen ranks the stellar position against
\NCtrl{} controls drawn from the same field. The smallest rank such an
ensemble can express is $1/(\NCtrl+1)=\RankFloorVal$, and that is a
statement about the resolution of the ensemble, not about the probability
that noise produced the window. Equation~(\ref{eq:bonf}) compares it with
the survey-wide Bonferroni scale $\alpha_{\rm survey}=0.05/\NWindows$ and
finds the floor \BonfRankRatio{} times too coarse. No single window can
therefore reach survey-level significance by rank alone, however extreme
it looks, and enlarging the control ensemble does not rescue this: the
primary beam bounds how many independent control positions a field
contains.

This paper recommends a visibility-domain statistic in several places, so
it is fair to ask what that would actually buy, and the survey's own data
answer it. Both statistics were computed for the same \VgNFlag{} stage-1
windows. In the image plane those \VgNFlag{} are indistinguishable: every
one has zero of the \NCtrl{} control positions at or above the star, so
every one sits at rank 1 of $\NCtrl+1$, the floor, equivalent to
\VgRankFloorZ$\sigma$ and with no dynamic range left to separate them. The
visibility fit separates the same \VgNFlag{} immediately. Windows carrying
real emission at the stellar position reach
\VgAtStarLo--\VgAtStarHi$\sigma$ on the real part, every unattributed
window is consistent with nothing at the star at below
\VgNullMax$\sigma$, and the gap between the two groups is
\VgSep$\sigma$. The strongest window reaches \VgVisMax$\sigma$, a factor
of \VgGain{} beyond anything the rank statistic can express whatever the
data contain. The gain is therefore not a marginal improvement in depth; it
is the difference between a statistic that saturates on every candidate it
finds and one that does not.

The consequence is the design adopted here. A technosignature claim must
rest on independent recurrence or independent astrophysical confirmation,
and the screen's role is to decide which windows are examined, not which
are real. It also sets a target for successor surveys. Raising the
resolution floor requires either many more independent control positions
per field than a single pointing affords, or a different statistic
altogether --- most naturally a visibility-domain test, which asks a
physical question about where the emission is and does not inherit this
floor at all.

\subsection{The null result has not been checked against an independent
pipeline}
\label{sec:nocross}

One limitation deserves the same prominence as the sensitivity caveats,
and it is not a limitation of the data. Every validation reported in this
paper tests this pipeline against itself. The injection campaign, the
false-alarm calibration, the radius correction and the region-maximum
audit all share a single codebase with the search, so a fault common to
the search and the injection code could in principle suppress a real
signal, or produce the appearance of a well-calibrated non-detection,
without any internal check being able to reveal it.

No external check is available. The only prior ALMA-archival
technosignature search \citep{Mason2024} targeted stars beyond 1\,kpc,
disjoint from this 40\,pc sample, so there is no shared data on which to
compare thresholds or dispositions. Neither an independent
re-implementation of the search statistic nor a blind third-party
injection has been performed. \textbf{The null result reported here
therefore rests on internal validation alone.}

What we can do, and have done, is remove our own code from the loop for
the one thing that can be specified analytically. The release includes a
set of known-answer test vectors generated from first principles ---
Gaussian noise plus a closed-form drifting sinusoid, with no import from
the search or injection modules --- together with the recovery each case
should produce. A reader who did not write this pipeline can therefore
check its stacking and normalisation against arithmetic rather than
against our tooling. A blind third-party injection into raw visibilities
remains the check we would most want, and we recommend it to anyone
extending this work.

\subsection{What this sample can and cannot speak for} \label{sec:bias}


Selection is on archival coverage, and ALMA's archive was not built
to sample stars. \NDiskCatStars{} of the \NStars{} searched stars were
observed under proposals categorised \emph{Disks and planet formation}
(Fig.~\ref{fig:selbias}), and
\NDebrisKwStars{} carry the \emph{Debris disks} science keyword
(\NDebrisKwSys{} of \NSystems{} systems). Among the \NTeffSample{} searched stars with a Gaia
temperature, \MNSample{} are M~dwarfs, \MPctSample{} per cent, against
\MPctCensus{} per cent of the reference census
(Table~\ref{tab:selfunc}), so the sample is F- and G-enriched and M-dwarf
deficient. Window counts are concentrated too: three systems ---
\ConcThreeNames{} --- carry \ConcThreeWin{} of the \NWindows{} searched
windows, \ConcThreePct{} per cent.

Two consequences
follow, and they run in opposite directions. A debris-disc-dominated sample is
the worst case for astrophysical false positives at millimetre wavelengths,
which is where the stage-1 flagged windows of \S\ref{sec:technosearch} concentrate, so the
vetting burden here is heavier than a blind sample would carry. And because
M~dwarfs host most of the habitable-zone planets in the solar
neighbourhood, and only \NMSearched{} of the \NMCensus{} within 40\,pc
carry a searched window: \PctMSearched{} per cent. This result
therefore carries very little demographic weight for the local
habitable-zone planet population. Both counts are restricted to the
temperature-classified sample of Table~\ref{tab:selfunc}, so they
illustrate the selection rather than measure it.

The point is sharper stated in targets than in percentages. Five of the
searched M~dwarfs host a planet that some published analysis places in the
habitable zone: Proxima~Centauri, TRAPPIST-1, LHS~1140, GJ~581 and HN~Lib.
Habitable-zone membership is model-dependent and two of these are
contested, so the number should be read as an order of magnitude and not a
census. Five is what this survey covers of the class of
target that most current technosignature programmes prioritise, against a
local M-dwarf population of \NMCensus{} within 40\,pc.
Conversely, the
over-representation of F and G stars means Sun-like stars are sampled
relatively heavily, though still through a highly non-random, disc-selected
archive. Either way it constrains transmitters around the stars ALMA happened
to point at, not the local stellar population.

Useful subsamples still fall out of archival selection. \NExoHostsTab{} of the
\NStars{} processed stars (\NExoPlanetsTab{} known planets) are confirmed
exoplanet hosts, among them Proxima~Centauri, $\tau$~Ceti, $\epsilon$~Eridani,
$\beta$~Pictoris and the seven-planet TRAPPIST-1 system, while HN~Lib and
LHS~1140, each with a habitable-zone planet, now carry an ALMA technosignature
non-detection. Four searched stars are white dwarfs
\citep{GentileFusillo2021}, all continuum non-detections and among the few
mm/submm SETI constraints for that class \citep{PollutedWD2026}.

\section{Conclusions}
\label{sec:conclusions}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=4pt,topsep=4pt,parsep=0pt]

\item \textbf{Search performed.} \NWinA{} fine-channel spectral windows
toward \NSysClassA{} independent stellar systems, covering
$\Delta\nu_A=\DnuA$\,GHz of unique sky frequency, searched for an
unresolved narrowband carrier whose frequency drifts. A secondary
coarse-channel spectral-excess search covered a further
$\Delta\nu_{B\rm -only}=\DnuBonly$\,GHz in \NWinB{} windows but cannot
discriminate drift. Both come from a uniform reduction of \LedProcessed{}
public execution blocks, \TotalDataTB\,TB of raw data.

\item \textbf{Sensitivity.} Measured by injection and recovery through the
complete automated selection, not inferred from a nominal threshold. Per
system, on each system's best window,
$P_{90}^{\rm sel}=\PromoteSysLoA$--$\PromoteSysHiA$\,W of equivalent
isotropic radiated power with a median of $\PromoteSysMedA$\,W. This is an
upper bound on reach rather than a central estimate: the window-to-window
transfer of the calibration is uncertain by
$\times\StratTransferNineLo$--$\times\StratTransferNineHi$ and dominates
the budget, and a one-sided $+\BudDecorLo$ to $+\BudDecorHi$ per cent
decorrelation bias can only make the figure optimistic.

\item \textbf{Result.} \NStageOneWin{} windows passed the automated
screen; \NStageOneLine{} are attributable to circumstellar or foreground
carbon monoxide, among them the $\beta$~Pictoris debris disc recovered
blind in two transitions, which serves as the survey's positive control.
\textbf{None of the remaining \NStageOneUnattrib{} is localised as a point
source at the stellar position in the visibilities, and none is
independently reproduced in a repeat observation.} The screen itself
assigns them no calibrated false-alarm probability, because its null
distribution is empirically non-uniform under both the frozen and the
radius-corrected normalisation (\S\ref{sec:radval}).

\item \textbf{Interpretation.} No persistent, independently confirmed
unresolved narrowband carrier was found within the frequency, drift-rate,
duty-cycle and sensitivity domain searched. \textbf{This cannot be
converted into an occurrence constraint on technological civilisations
within 40\,pc.} The sample is archive-selected and strongly incomplete:
\PctDiscProposal{} per cent of the searched stars come from disc and
planet-formation programmes, only \NMSearched{} of \NMCensus{} catalogued
M~dwarfs within 40\,pc are covered, and the median system is searched over
\SysUnionAMed\,GHz rather than the survey's \DnuA\,GHz union. No occurrence figure, hosting fraction or
figure of merit is quoted in the results, the discussion or these
conclusions. Those quantities require a denominator this sample does not
supply, so they are confined to Appendix~\ref{app:cwtfm}, where they are
given for comparison with surveys that report them and are conditional on
this sample, these frequencies and these epochs, and on nothing wider.

\end{enumerate}
