(coarse), \DwellFineT--\DwellFineOne{} per cent (fine), over
$f_{\rm dwell}=0.10$--1.00.}

\input{tab_compcurve}
\end{table}

The drift ceiling carries one caveat. The searched value is
\DriftCeilLo--\DriftCeilHi\,Hz\,s$^{-1}$\,GHz$^{-1}$, line-of-sight
$|a|=\AccelCeilLo$--\AccelCeilHi\,m\,s$^{-2}$, raised above that generic
value only in the \NAccelPlanet{} windows where a known planet's own bound
requires it. It clears Earth-rotation and Earth-orbit drifts by two orders
of magnitude but is marginal against a close-in planet on a
TRAPPIST-1\,b-like orbit, so the limits of \S\ref{sec:discussion} are
conditional on it (Figure~\ref{fig:accel}).

\emph{Interference rejection} is an operative gate. The highest frequencies
allocated to any satellite constellation are the E-band feeder links of the
second-generation systems, 71--76 and 81--86\,GHz \citep{Starlink2024},
below the \UnionLo\,GHz start of the searched union, and ITU Radio
Regulations No.~5.340 prohibits all emissions in bands covering
\ItuGHz\,GHz (\ItuPct{} per cent) of it. Allocation tables exclude neither
harmonics nor intermodulation products nor near-field emitters, so no
exclusion claim rests on one; the operative defences are the control
ensemble, the visibility phase test and recurrence, measured in
Appendix~\ref{app:rfi}.

\emph{What was searched}, by class. Class~A, the fine-channel
drifting-carrier search: \DomAIslands{} frequency islands totalling
\DomAUnion\,GHz between \DomAFreqLo{} and \DomAFreqHi\,GHz, channels
\DomAChanLo--\DomAChanHi\,MHz, accelerations
$|a|\leq\DomAAccelLo$--\DomAAccelHi\,m\,s$^{-2}$. Class~B, persistent
channel-confined excess with no drift discrimination: \DomBIslands{} islands
totalling $\Delta\nu_B=\DnuB$\,GHz between \DomBFreqLo{} and
\DomBFreqHi\,GHz, overlapping Class~A by \DnuOverlap\,GHz and so adding
$\Delta\nu_{B\rm -only}=\DnuBonly$\,GHz, channels
\DomBChanLo--\DomBChanHi\,MHz. Both cover only the archival epochs and only
carriers one native channel or narrower, over \SearchedUnionGHz\,GHz of the
\UnionGHz\,GHz union after molecular-line masking, at nominal trigger
thresholds whose measured recovery is
$\RecThresh^{+\RecThreshHi}_{-\RecThreshLo}$ per cent. Inside that box the
non-detection is a constraint. Outside it, the exclusions are those of
\S\ref{sec:excludes}: the trigger is not a significance; the spatial screen
is empirically calibrated, and holds for isolated point sources rather than
for beams filled by resolved circumstellar or foreground emission; the
\NCoarse{} coarse windows carry no drift discrimination and no
survey-threshold completeness curve; coverage is the \UnionIntervals{}
islands of Fig.~\ref{fig:waterfall} less the \MaskUnionPct{} per cent of the
union removed by the $\pm\MaskVWidth$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ line mask; drift above
the ceiling and emission broader than one native channel lie outside the
searched morphology; duty cycles below ${\sim}0.1$ are essentially
unconstrained; and the search is Stokes-$I$ only, so a per-hand search of
the \NEbBothHands{} blocks that deliver both parallel hands would gain up to
$\times\PolGain$ on a polarised carrier.

\subsection{Amplitude completeness from visibility-domain injections}
\label{sec:ampinject}

Synthetic tones of known amplitude were injected into real visibilities,
using the extraction code's own mechanism as far upstream of the statistic
as the retained products allow, and the unmodified pipeline's return
measured. Six configurations span three bands and both classes: three coarse
Band~7 windows of AT~Mic, one coarse window each in Bands~3 and~6 of AU~Mic,
and AU~Mic's 488-kHz flagband window with its 49-trial drift grid. Each
receives a complex tone at the star position at $4$--$10\times$ the
statistic's own noise, over drift fractions $f_{\rm drift}=0$--$1.0$ of the
ceiling, on- and half-grid rates and channel-centred and boundary
placements: 200 trials per configuration, \NTrials{} in all. The recovery
criterion was frozen in advance: $T\geq5$, peak channel within 3 of the
injection, peak drift within one grid step.

Coarse windows recover 0 of 500 injections from $T_\star=4$ to $10$ under
that criterion, which they cannot satisfy, every in-grid drift being
sub-channel over the track so that the reported peak drift is arbitrary; the
fine window fails it the same way. Scored on detection alone the same
windows recover essentially everything
(Appendix~\ref{app:dwelldetail}). Class~B limits therefore bound carriers of
any dwell fraction, with the completeness of \S\ref{sec:dwell}; what those
windows lack is drift discrimination.

On the fine window, drifting carriers ($f_{\rm drift}\geq0.5$, pooled over
grid phase and sub-channel placement) are recovered 17, \RecThresh, 58, 75
and \RecTwice{} per cent at $T_\star=4$, 5, 6, 8 and 10, saturating by twice
threshold power (Fig.~\ref{fig:completeness}). With the tone injected after
the baseline step the machinery alone is amplitude-faithful to within
$-9$/$+14$ per cent for channel-centred tones. Boundary-straddling placement
costs $0.6$--$0.8$ in the statistic and recovers 0 per cent at the trigger
against 83 per cent for centred tones, crossing 50 per cent near
$1.4\times$ threshold amplitude. Survey completeness uses the
placement-pooled curve, conservative for the boundary-straddling case:
EIRP$_{50}=1.10\,\mathrm{EIRP}_{\rm trig}$ and
EIRP$_{90}>2.0\,\mathrm{EIRP}_{\rm trig}$, a bound only.

That curve is measured on one window and is not a survey completeness
function. Of the \CfgNA{} Class~A windows, \CfgAxesZeroOne{}
(\CfgAxesZeroOnePct{} per cent) differ from the calibration configuration on
at most one axis, the mismatch dominated by band (\CfgBandOut{} windows) and
drift-trial count (\CfgDriftOut) rather than channel width (\CfgChanOut),
integration time (\CfgOnSrcOut) or beam offset (\CfgOffOut). Those are the
axes the stratified campaign spans; other target environments and
edge-phased drift grids are not spanned at all.

\emph{The stratified campaign.} A drifting carrier was injected into the
retained per-integration spectra of \StratWindows{} further windows, one per
star, spanning Bands~\StratBandLo--\StratBandHi,
\StratChanLo--\StratChanHi\,kHz channels, \StratIntLo--\StratIntHi{}
integrations, \StratDriftLo--\StratDriftHi{} drift trials and both the 12\,m
array and the ACA, and pushed through the pipeline's own baseline removal
and de-drift stack on the search's drift grid: nine amplitudes,
\StratTrials{} trials. Recovery is a threshold crossing at the injected
frequency and not the global maximum of the grid, since requiring an
injection to beat a window's brightest cell measures that window's worst
artefact. Pooled recovery is \StratRecFour, \StratRecFive, \StratRecSix{}
and \StratRecEight{} per cent at $T_\star=4$, 5, 6 and 8, with
$P_{50}=\StratPFifty\sigma$ and $P_{90}=\StratPNinety\sigma$
(Fig.~\ref{fig:stratcomp}). The spread across configurations sets the
transfer bracket: $P_{50}$ runs
$\StratTransferLo$--$\StratTransferHi\times$ its median and $P_{90}$
$\StratTransferNineLo$--$\StratTransferNineHi\times$ its own, so the assumed
$0.5$--$2\times$ bracket is conservative. Band and array matter least,
$P_{50}$ varying only
$\StratPFiftyBandLo$--$\StratPFiftyBandHi\sigma$ across bands
(Table~\ref{tab:driftstrata}).

\emph{Drift rate, resolved.} All \DriftStratTrials{} trials carry a non-zero
drift, drawn over $\DriftStratRateLo$--$\DriftStratRateHi$\,Hz\,s$^{-1}$, so
the pooled curve is a drifting-carrier curve. A pooled curve could hide a
deficit at the edge of the grid, where a carrier crosses the most channels
and the de-drift stack has the least signal in any one of them, so
Table~\ref{tab:driftstrata} splits the campaign by drift rate as a fraction
of each window's own ceiling. $P_{90}$ varies by \DriftStratSpread{} per
cent across the three thirds with no trend, and the outer third is not the
worst; at a fixed $\DriftStratFixAmp\sigma$ injection they recover
\DriftStratLowRecSix, \DriftStratMidRecSix{} and \DriftStratHighRecSix{} per
cent. The rate is recovered as well as the frequency:
\DriftStratMatchPct{} per cent of detections above $\DriftStratFixAmp\sigma$
return the injected drift trial itself, median error \DriftStratErrMed{}
grid steps and \DriftStratErrNine{} at the ninetieth percentile. Carrier
sub-channel phase, stratified separately over \PhaseStratLevels{} positions
and \PhaseStratTrials{} trials, is the one axis that does matter, and is why
the threshold carries the
$\times\PhaseStratBest$--$\times\PhaseStratWorst$ response correction rather
than a single factor.

\emph{How far the grid reaches.} \StratWindows{} configurations are a sparse
grid for \NWinA{} windows. It spans Bands~\GridBands, channel widths
\GridChanLo--\GridChanHi\,kHz, \GridNintLo--\GridNintHi{} integrations and
both arrays, and every Class~A window lies in a band it samples. The
configuration needed for the comparison --- band, channel width and
integration count together --- is recorded for \GridKnownN{} of the \NWinA;
the peak-frequency freeze that carries the integration count does not cover
the \GridNoNint{} blocks searched since, which are never assumed to be
inside the grid. Of the \GridKnownN{} that can be judged, \GridInside{} lie
inside the grid on all three axes at once, \GridInsidePct{} per cent of them
and \GridInsidePctAll{} per cent of all \NWinA. The remaining
\GridOutside{} are extrapolations, \GridOutNint{} in integration count alone
and \GridOutChan{} in channel width. The transfer bracket is measured for
the \GridInside{} windows and assumed for the rest. The five deepest Class~A
thresholds are all interpolated, so the headline sensitivity does not rest
on an extrapolation.

\begin{figure*}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.96\textwidth]{figures/stratified_completeness.pdf}
\caption{Stratified injection recovery. Grey, each of the \StratWindows{}
configurations separately; blue, the pooled curve over \StratTrials{} trials.
Dotted lines mark the 50 and 90 per cent levels. The spread between grey
curves is the completeness transfer, measured rather than assumed. Right,
recovery against drift fraction of the searched ceiling at $T_\star=5$
and~$8$.}
\label{fig:stratcomp}
\label{fig:completeness}
\end{figure*}

\emph{The spectral response, measured.} The $\times\HanFacMed$ correction of
\S\ref{sec:method} is analytic, the injected tones being unsmoothed. A tone
injected at fractional position $\phi$, split between neighbouring channels
as the delivered channelisation requires and convolved with the
$0.25/0.5/0.25$ kernel, has a peak $\times\HanMeasCentred$ that of a
channel-centred unsmoothed tone of equal total flux at $\phi=0$ and
$\times\HanMeasEdge$ at the channel edge, median $\times\HanMeasMed$ over
\HanMeasTrials{} trials, every point within \HanMeasDevPct{} per cent of the
analytic value. Both campaigns ship trial-level records.

\subsection{Dwell campaign: end-to-end check, scope and representativeness}
\label{app:dwelldetail}

A zero-drift, always-on tone injected into the calibrated visibilities of a
coarse Band~7 window at 1, 3 and 10 times the per-integration rms returns
$T_\star = 20.5$, $65.3$ and $217.7$, each within one channel of the
injection. The ideal stacking gain over its 639 integrations is
$\sqrt{639}=25$, and the measured 20.5 is that value less weighting and
flagging losses. This is both the end-to-end check through the unmodified
pipeline and what a coarse window returns when detection alone is scored:
Class~B detects excess power, and cannot tell whether the excess drifted.

Two distinctions apply. The dwell campaign injects into retained
per-integration spectra, after the baseline step, while the end-to-end check
injects into visibilities before it; and one cell, the through-pipeline
recovery of near-static carriers on Class~A windows, is measured only in
part.

How representative the injected windows are of the survey is set out in
\S\ref{sec:ampinject}: the end-to-end demonstration is made on one
configuration, about half the Class~A windows differ from it on at most one
axis, and the stratified campaign measures the transfer to the rest and
states which windows that bracket interpolates and which it extrapolates.

The injected carriers do not drift, so what is measured across \CompWin{}
configurations is the persistent and partial-dwell non-drifting class. On
Class~B windows that is the searched class; on Class~A windows the searched
class is the drifting carrier, whose curve rests on the single calibration
configuration of \S\ref{sec:ampinject}. The stacked amplitude $a$ is a
derived combination, justified by the form of the statistic and by the
collapse of \CompWin{} configurations onto one curve, not measured
independently at every $(A,f_{\rm dwell})$ pair.

