\section{The velocity-space line mask} \label{app:linemask}
\input{tab_maskband}

A one-channel line check is too tight for circumstellar gas: a native
channel spans a fraction of a km\,s$^{-1}$, while real emission is
shifted by many channels (\S\ref{sec:technosearch}). The mask tolerance is set by the kinematic budget of circumstellar gas:
Keplerian motion where these molecules survive
($\lesssim$13\,km\,s$^{-1}$ beyond 10\,au), linewidths
$\lesssim$1\,km\,s$^{-1}$, and an uncorrected barycentric term bounded
by $\pm$30\,km\,s$^{-1}$. The result is empirically conservative: the largest
attributed circumstellar component ($\beta$~Pic, within
\BpicMaxAbsStel\,km\,s$^{-1}$ of systemic) sits at
\BpicMaskPct{} per cent of it, and \emph{zero}
crossing frequencies lie 13--50\,km\,s$^{-1}$ from their nearest
\emph{masked} transition (the wider catalogues are another matter:
\S\ref{sec:technosearch}), so a $\pm13$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ Keplerian-only mask would have
suppressed and released the same windows: the wider tolerance costs no
yield and buys robustness against radial-velocity catalogue error.
The alternative selection quoted in \S\ref{sec:linecost} is every Splatalogue
transition over the \UnionIntervals{} islands with $E_{\rm u}\le\MaskDbEuK$\,K
and $\log A_{ij}\ge\MaskDbLogA$, \MaskDbTrans{} of \MaskDbCarriers{} carriers;
HCN, CS, SiO, CN and H$_2$CO are in the adopted list already, and no coverage
figure and no disposition changes.

\emph{The repaired mask.} The four defects and their repair are in
\S\ref{sec:linecost}. The rounding defect is why the released
CO($1{\to}0$) offset column,
referred to the frozen mask's rounded \CatCOoneGHz\,GHz entry, reads
\CatCOerrMHz\,MHz (\CatCOerrKms\,km\,s$^{-1}$) smaller than the
laboratory-referred value of Table~\ref{tab:bpicaudit}. A cheaper rule exists: the archive records the rest frequency each
execution block was tuned to, so masking each window's own tuned line, inside
an LSR-frame tube of a few $\times10$\,km\,s$^{-1}$, catches the
$\beta$~Pictoris, HD~48370 and [C\,\textsc{i}] windows automatically.

The crossing ledger, once: of the \NHits{} windows with an on-star
crossing, \NSpatial{} also beat their control ring and three of those
are accounted for by the mask. Two further crossing windows fall inside
the mask without beating their ring (the unflagged $\beta$~Pic
Class~\BpRecSixClass{} window at \StelBpicCoarse\,km\,s$^{-1}$; HD~285968 at $+8.7$\,km\,s$^{-1}$,
already rejected by its ring, maximum 10.9 against a star peak of 6.5),
and the remainder lie far outside any circumstellar tolerance (AU~Mic
$+379$, LHS~1140 $+115$\,km\,s$^{-1}$). The other
\NonExcRingGe{} are ordinary noise crossings: in each, the window's own
control ring reaches or exceeds the stellar value (ring-maximum median
\NonExcRingMed{} against a crossing median \NonExcHitMed), the expected
Gaussian-tailed population of moderate $T_\star=5$--$5.5$ crossings with
comparably strong peaks among \NCtrl{} controls. But the transitions
that make astrophysical false positives cheap are also natural reference
frequencies for a deliberate communicator \citep{CocconiMorrison1959},
so the released products keep a known-line-coincident list: every on-source threshold crossing inside the masked intervals, with its offset from each
nearby transition.

\section{The ancillary continuum lane}
\label{app:continuum}
\label{app:contsearch}

The continuum lane is an exploratory by-product: 57 measurements, 17
detections and 40 limits. A millimetre excess is at best an indirect and
model-dependent observable of waste-heat technology. An ``upper limit'' here
is $5\times$ the primary-beam-corrected map rms at the star's position, and
the deepest non-detection is towards TRAPPIST-1 in Band~3. Under the combined
error model sixteen of the seventeen detections stay above the trigger, the exception being $\eta$~Corvi Band~7 at $T_\star=4.7$.

Every detection is astrophysically unsurprising: the unresolved UV~Ceti
binary; six systems of disc or chromospheric emission ($\beta$~Pic, AU~Mic,
AT~Mic and $\tau$~Cet among them); six stars at photospheric flux; and two
late-M dwarfs of documented magnetic activity, LP~349$-$25 and the auroral
emitter LSR~J1835$+$3259 \citep{Hallinan2015}. Closure phase discriminates
structure for none of them at these flux densities.

\emph{The anomaly screen.} Each star's flux is tested by a robust median/MAD
statistic against its own photospheric expectation and against same-$T_{\rm
eff}$ stars in coarse bins of at least five. The error model, fixed before any
flag, adds the map noise, a calibration term of 5 per cent in Bands~3--6 and
10 per cent in Bands~7--8, and a model term propagated from the Gaia
temperature and radius errors. It has \emph{no intrinsic-variability term}.
That is adequate for a photosphere and wrong for an active M dwarf, whose
millimetre flux can vary by factors of several, so the screen is uninformative
for exactly the stars a radio-stellar reader cares about. It flags for
inspection and tests no per-star hypothesis. One outlier results, $\chi^1$~Ori
at Band~3, consistent with a spectroscopic G0V binary's unresolved companion
or a chromosphere.

\section{False-alarm accounting}
\label{app:falsealarm}

\begin{table*}
\centering
\scriptsize
\caption{\textbf{Every false-alarm expectation quoted in this paper, and
what each one predicts.} The first six predict the number of windows in
which a no-signal position would \emph{rank first} among the control
ensemble; they differ in sample, denominator and whether the measured tail
excess and the within-block clustering are included, and they are pipeline
diagnostics. A stage-1 event also requires the position to reach the
trigger, so only the last row, which imposes that condition, may be
compared with an observed count; it is the comparison used in the abstract,
in \S\ref{sec:technosearch} and in the conclusions. Square brackets give a
bootstrap interval on the \emph{expectation}, round brackets the 95 per cent
interval of the predicted \emph{count} under that expectation.}
\label{tab:falsealarm}
\input{tab_falsealarm_v395}
\end{table*}

Table~\ref{tab:falsealarm} collects every false-alarm expectation quoted in
this paper. Only the last row conditions on the trigger, and it alone is
compared with an observed count (\S\ref{sec:technosearch}); the other six
predict rank-first windows and are diagnostics of the pipeline.

The per-window rank-first rate is $1/\RankFloor$ and the survey expectation
$\NWindows/\RankFloor=\ExpFlags$. The floor falls only if the control
ensemble grows, which the primary beam bounds. Windows are not independent
trials either, grouping into \NEB{} execution blocks that share weather,
calibration and correlator setup. Drawing one control index per block and
applying it to all of that block's windows gives the clustered distribution
in closed form: mean \ClustMean{} rank-first windows, and under the trigger
\ClustStageMean{} stage-1 flagged windows with $P(\geq1)=\ClustStagePone$ for
the single unattributed one. Clustering changes little because the
\emph{position} of the control maximum is not shared across a block's
windows.

Table~\ref{tab:excess} gives the trials budget in cells and decomposes the
on-star excess. A window holds \CellsWinMin--\CellsWinMax{}
channel$\times$drift cells (median \CellsWinMed), the survey \CellsTotal{} in
total. Correlation cannot move the expected \emph{number} above the trigger,
which is $Np$ whatever the correlation, only its clumping, so the excess over
an iid-Gaussian budget decomposes into two multiplicative parts: cell
multiplicity within a crossing window, a factor \CellsPerCrossRatio{} which
is what an over-count of \FaFreqOver{} on the frequency axis predicts, one
real threshold excursion being counted about three times; and the count of
crossing windows, a factor \CrossWinRatio{} whose one-sided Poisson
probability is \CrossWinPoisson. Once the four dispositioned windows are set
aside, the residual excess is consistent with chance at that level.

The control ensembles calibrate the budget without a Gaussian assumption. The
same arithmetic predicts \NCtrlWinExp{} of the \NWindows{} windows carrying a
control above $T_{\rm trig}$ and \NCtrlWinObs{} do, agreeing to
\CtrlWinAgreePct{} per cent, with per-window control maxima tracking the
iid-Gaussian prediction at a median ratio \CtrlMaxRatioMed{} (5th--95th
percentile \CtrlMaxRatioLo--\CtrlMaxRatioHi). Grid cells are not independent
trials: Hanning smoothing correlates neighbouring channels at
$\rho_1=\HanRhoOneFrac$ and $\rho_2=\HanRhoTwoFrac$, and the drift grid is
oversampled two to one, giving the combined over-count \FaOverCount{} of
Table~\ref{tab:excess}. Correcting for it leaves the observed control maxima
$+\FaExcessFine$ and $+\FaExcessCoarse$ per cent above prediction in the fine
and coarse classes independently. Non-Gaussianity of that size is why the
operative statement is the empirical rank and not the Gaussian budget. The
residual on-star excess is not zero, runs one way and carries the same sign
at the controls, so it is reported as an open systematic: the crossing list
is dominated by CO($2{\to}1$) at 230.5\,GHz toward disc hosts, and a residual
excess of chance crossings at the tens-of-per-cent level cannot be excluded.

