All of this fixes what a single window can ever mean. The rank floor is
$1/(N_{\rm ctrl}+1)=\RankFloorVal$ against a survey-wide Bonferroni scale of
$\BonfScaleCalc$, a factor \BonfRankRatio, and correlation widens it: with
$N_{\rm eff}\simeq30$--$43$ independent spatial trials the ensemble resolves
only \RankEffLo--\RankEffHi. Drawing more controls would not help, the
primary beam bounding the annulus. This is the design's central limitation,
and is why authentication rests on recurrence and astrophysical attribution
rather than on rank.

The control geometry uses a 12-m $\theta_{\rm PB}$ for every window, so the
\NWinSevenM{} ACA 7-m windows carry a known geometric error. It runs in the
conservative direction: on the true 7-m beam those annuli span
\AcaAnnFracLo--\AcaAnnFracHi{} of $\theta_{\rm PB}$ at a median area-weighted
gain of \AcaAnnGain{} against \RingGainMean{} for the 12-m windows, so
exchangeability in flux density improves there. Table~\ref{tab:arraysplit}
gives the two strata side by side. The 12\,m stratum holds
\NStageOneTwelveM{} of the \NSpatial{} stage-1 flagged windows, the one lost
being HD~48370's foreground CO, and the deepest trigger and the deepest
$P_{\rm eff}$ are both 12\,m windows, so no survey-level statement changes.
Re-extracting the ACA windows on the correct 7\,m beam would remove the error
rather than bound it, and needs the raw visibilities and a full re-search.
Excluding the \NBPWindows{} $\beta$~Pictoris windows leaves one unattributed
crossing among \NNonBP, against \ExpNonBP{} expected
($P(\geq1)=\PgeOneNonBPPct$ per cent).

