The rank distribution of the remaining \HONoiseWin{} windows is adverse to the
method. The star's median add-one rank is \HoRankMed{} where 0.5 is expected,
with \HOFracLoPct{} per cent of ranks below 0.1 and \HOFracHiPct{} per cent
above 0.9 against 10 per cent each, so the distribution is displaced rather
than growing a tail. The window-level figure ($D=\HOKsD$, $p\HOKsP$) treats
windows sharing a calibration as independent, but clustering removes neither
the shift nor its significance: \HOBlockBelow{} of \HOBlockN{} blocks have a
mean rank below 0.5 (sign test $p\,\HOBlockSignP$), and a star-clustered
bootstrap over \HOBootWin{} windows places the median at \HOBootMed{} (95 per
cent \HOBootLo--\HOBootHi). The survey's own \NWindows{} windows lean the same
way without resolving it (median \SurvMedP, star-clustered 95 per cent
\SurvBootLo--\SurvBootHi) and the two distributions are indistinguishable
($D=\SurvHOKsD$, $p=\SurvHOKsP$), so the in-sample checks were underpowered
rather than contradicted. The control ensemble is an empirically calibrated
screen for extreme values, not an exactly exchangeable rank test.

\subsubsection{The radial gradient}
\label{app:radialfull}

The cause is the noise scale. Images are corrected for the primary-beam
response, so the true noise rises with distance from the pointing centre,
while the search divides every position by one scale $\sigma(t,\nu)$ estimated
from the probe ensemble as a whole (\S\ref{sec:statistic}). Positions at large
radius are divided by too small a scale and stand too high; the star, inside
the annulus at \RadRIn\,$\theta_{\rm PB}$, is divided by too large a scale and
stands too low. That is the sign observed, and extended emission on short
baselines adds to it.

Three tests establish it, all from the released products. The control probes
are drawn once from a fixed seed, so fractional radii are identical in every
window and each stored control statistic pairs with its radius; the star's
median offset from the pointing centre, \RadUStarMed$\,\theta_{\rm PB}$, is an
order of magnitude inside the annulus. \emph{First}, the control level depends
on radius: in units of each window's own robust noise scale the pooled control
statistic runs \RadProfIn{} at $u=\RadProfUIn$, falls to \RadProfMin{} near
mid-annulus and returns to \RadProfOut{} at the outer edge (\RadProfInHO,
\RadProfMinHO{} and \RadProfOutHO{} in the calibration sample).
\emph{Second}, matching on radius removes most of the departure: ranking the
star against only the \RadInnerN{} probes with $u\leq\RadInnerUHi$ moves the
survey median from \RadRankAllMed{} to \RadRankInMed{} and $D=\RadRankAllD$
($p=\RadRankAllP$) to \RadRankInD{} ($p=\RadRankInP$), and the \RadInsideN{}
windows whose star falls inside the annulus have median rank \RadInsideMed{}
against \RadOutsideMed{} for the \RadOutsideN{} that do not. \emph{Third}, a
position with no star in it behaves the same way: an innermost probe treated
as a \emph{pseudo-star} and ranked against the other \NCtrl$-1$ probes is
displaced as far as the real ones out of sample, median \HOPseudoMed{} against
\HORadRankAllMed{} ($p=\HOPseudoP$), though not within the survey
($\RadPseudoMed$, $p=\RadPseudoP$). The displacement is a property of
\emph{position in the field}, not of stars.

The repair belongs at the measurement stage: standardising each probe within
its own radius bin, and the star on the innermost bin, removes the gradient by
construction, with no free parameter and no stellar debit. Run over all
\LocAllWin{} windows it changes the list --- \LocStageBoth{} windows are
stage-1 flagged under both statistics, \LocStageLost{} lose the flag
(\LocLostList) and \LocStageGained{} gains it (\LocGainedList), so the count
falls from \LocStageGlobal{} to \LocStageLocal. The four that fall out are
marginal cases already dispositioned on other grounds; the one that enters,
HD~207129, is a debris-disc host whose crossing lies
$\HdTwoZeroSevenDv$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ from CO($2\to1$) in the stellar frame.

We do not adopt the radius-corrected statistic as the primary one, for an
empirical reason: applied prospectively to \RadValStarN{} windows it played no
part in building, it leaves the stellar rank inconsistent with uniformity and
moves the median \RadValStarToward{} the exchangeable value
(\S\ref{sec:radval}), while the survey median goes from \LocAllMedGlobal{} to
\LocAllMedLocal, slightly further from 0.5. Since the correction removes the
radial component of the displacement by construction, what is left is larger
than what it removed and is not radial. The frozen statistic therefore defines
every count reported here; under the corrected one the survey would have
\LocCorrStage{} stage-1 flagged windows, \LocCorrAttrib{} attributed to
circumstellar or foreground CO and \LocCorrUnattrib{} unattributed ---
different windows, the same conclusion --- and the released catalogue carries
both per window. A successor should fold the primary-beam response into the
per-position noise model from the start.

The same mechanism touches completeness, which the injections alone would not
reveal because they are placed at the stellar position only. Extrapolating the
out-of-sample radial profile to the stellar radius gives an offset of
\HoRadAtStar$\,\sigma$, so the scale applied at the star is too \emph{large}
and an injected signal is divided by too much: the quoted recovery fractions
are \HoRadTrendSign{} by of order \HoRadBiasPct{} per cent of the threshold,
not optimistic --- small, in the safe direction, but not zero.

The lesson is structural rather than particular to this survey: spatial
control positions prioritise candidates soundly in targeted interferometric
archival data but cannot establish significance in them, because the field
around a chosen target is not blank sky and the primary-beam response and
imaging weights vary across the annulus the controls occupy. The remedy is
physical rather than statistical --- a test for a point source at the stellar
position, which the visibilities answer directly (\S\ref{app:futurework}) ---
and any search ranking a source against positions in its own field should
measure the displacement out of sample first.

\subsubsection{The line mask}
\label{app:linecostfull}

The searches as executed matched crossings to catalogued rest frequencies
within one spectral channel at zero drift. The $\beta$~Pictoris windows showed
that inadequate: both fell outside that tolerance, by 10.6 and 21.9\,MHz,
while lying within 3.6\,km\,s$^{-1}$ of the systemic velocity of a resolved,
mapped disc. Every crossing was therefore reclassified with a velocity-space
mask, $\pm\MaskVWidth$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ in the stellar frame around each
catalogued transition (\S\ref{sec:linecost}; Appendix~\ref{app:linemask}) --- a
post-hoc classification criterion, never a detection threshold, whose
half-width was adopted after the $\beta$~Pictoris crossings had been seen.
Reclassifying all \NHits{} crossings at $\pm20$ to $\pm100$\,km\,s$^{-1}$
costs \MaskBWTwenty{} to \MaskBWHundred{} per cent of gross bandwidth and
admits no new unattributed outlier. That test is one-sided, since widening the
tube can only mask more, so it cannot show that the attributions survive a
\emph{narrower} tube; they do not rest on the half-width, each attributed
event being identified by stellar-frame velocity agreement through the full
frame chain (Table~\ref{tab:bpicaudit}), by an independently published
detection of the same line in the same source, or by recurrence across
execution blocks.

The mask holds \MaskNTransNew{} transitions of \MaskNSpecNew{} species at full
JPL/CDMS precision \citep{Pickett1998,CDMS2005}, applied in the LSR frame as
well as the stellar frame and re-applied to the frozen crossing list as a
post-hoc filter. The species criterion is the one objective rule statable in
advance: the transitions ALMA's band definitions are built around, namely the
CO isotopologues, HCN, HCO$^{+}$, CS, SiO, SO, CN, H$_2$CO and
[C\,\textsc{i}], which dominate the circumstellar and foreground emission of
disc-selected fields. The choice is consequential. A full Splatalogue
selection would mask \MaskDbPctUnion{} per cent of the union against the
\MaskUnionPct{} per cent the adopted list masks, and would place
\NCrossWithCat{} of the \NCrossQueried{} crossings whose peak frequency the
release records inside a tube (Appendix~\ref{app:linemask}), so ``coincident
with a known molecular line'' is a statement about a catalogue rather than a
binary property; since no masked crossing exceeds its own control ensemble
(\S\ref{sec:linecost}), no plausible widening or narrowing of the species list
changes a disposition.

The cost, measured on every JPL catalogued transition of the \MaskNSpecOld{}
frozen species displaced by each system's radial velocity, given a
$\pm50$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ stellar-frame half-width and intersected with each
window, is \MaskUnionPct{} per cent of the unique-frequency union and
\MaskGrossPct{} per cent of the window-summed gross bandwidth, CN and CO
dominating. Total exposure is $\ExpoSGHzNew$\,s\,GHz, or \ExpoStarHrGHz{}
star-hour-GHz, with \ExpoTopPct{} per cent of it in the five best-populated
2-GHz bins, which any frequency-prior calculation must weight
(Appendix~\ref{app:table}). The loss is not uniform, so Table~\ref{tab:maskband}
gives it band by band: it reaches \MaskWorstPct{} per cent in
Band~\MaskWorstBand{} and stays below 1.5 per cent in Bands~3--5. Nor is it
shared equally between the two experiments, \MaskLostA\,GHz of the
\MaskUnionA\,GHz Class~A union (\MaskPctA{} per cent) against \MaskLostB\,GHz
of \MaskUnionB\,GHz in Class~B (\MaskPctB{} per cent), so the fine-channel
experiment pays three times the fractional price, its coverage being
concentrated in the line-tuned windows a molecular mask is designed to remove.
The mask governs dispositions and leaves the noise-derived thresholds alone,
and 131 of \NWindows{} windows contain an in-band catalogued transition of the
masked species without a crossing falling within one native channel of a rest
frequency.

Masking is a design selection effect separating \emph{discovery} from
\emph{classification}. A carrier at a line frequency is as detectable as
anywhere else, but it cannot be classified, because the astrophysical prior at
a catalogued transition is overwhelming and this pipeline cannot separate the
two hypotheses on morphology alone. Removing those frequencies from the
searched domain is the conservative choice, at the price that \emph{we place
no constraint at all on artificial emission lying inside the masked regions,
at any signal strength}: every limit in this paper is conditional on that
exclusion. A better design searches every frequency and attaches a
\emph{lower prior} to a line-coincident event instead, since these are exactly
the frequencies a communicator wanting to be found near a natural reference
might choose. The released channels support that second search, in which a
crossing is admitted only on independent evidence of localisation, line
morphology, polarisation, recurrence or non-thermal width, and for which the
$\beta$~Pictoris crossings are the test case.

