\clearpage
\section{Recommendations for a next-generation ALMA technosignature search}
\label{app:futurework}
\label{app:instrument}

ALMA's advantages here are its mm-wave sensitivity, an essentially
unsearched tuning range, the interferometric spatial discrimination that
supplies the control statistic of \S\ref{sec:statistic}, and a public
archive re-processable without new allocations. Six changes would enlarge
what this experiment can say. They are given in priority order, and the
criterion is explicit: how much of the signal space each buys per unit of
effort, counting an archival reprocessing as cheap and new observing time
as expensive. The first two would change a conclusion; the last four would
widen a domain.

\emph{(1) Use the visibilities} [changes what a candidate means] This is the principal methodological lesson of
the survey, and \S\ref{sec:radial} is why. A celestial point source at the
stellar direction $\mathbf{x}_\star$ contributes
\begin{equation}
V_{ij}(\nu,t)=A(\nu,t)\,
\exp\!\left(-2\pi i\,\mathbf{u}_{ij}\!\cdot\!\mathbf{x}_\star\right),
\label{eq:visphase}
\end{equation}
so rotating the phase centre to the star should leave a coherent signal
entirely in the real part, with an imaginary component consistent with zero on
every baseline. That test
asks whether the data contain a point source \emph{where the star is}; the
control ranking asks only whether one sky extraction is unusually large
compared with neighbouring ones, which is a weaker question and, as
\S\ref{sec:radial} shows, one whose null depends on where in the field the
comparison positions sit. Applied to the one unattributed window
(\S\ref{sec:technosearch}) the visibility fit was decisive; applied to every
window it would demote the control ensemble to a calibration layer, remove the
radius-matching problem entirely, and give each candidate a physically
specific test rather than a rank. Doing so needs the visibilities retained,
which this survey did not do, and it is the first change we would make, which is why it is ranked first here.

\emph{(2) Search the remaining public blocks these stars already hold}
[multiplies the epochs on a minority of systems, at no telescope cost]
This recommendation has since been acted on in part, and
\S\ref{sec:extension} reports the result: \ExtBlocks{} of the
\LedNotProcessed{} blocks have been searched with the frozen pipeline.
They cost no telescope time and no new proposal. What they buy needs stating precisely, because it
is narrower than it sounds. Every one of them is repeat coverage
of a star and a tuning already searched, so they add no star, no system
and no frequency: the only axis they extend is time. They are also
concentrated, not spread --- \NExtraBlocksUnsearched{} of them attach to
\NStarUnsearchedEB{} of the \NStars{} stars and
\NSysUnsearchedEB{} of the \NSystems{} systems, with
Proxima~Centauri, TRAPPIST-1 and HD~202628 accounting for the largest
holdings. And they barely touch the confirmation gap that limits this survey: of
the \NSysOneEB{} systems with a single searched epoch, only
\NSysOneEBGain{} would acquire a second block. The extension bears that
out, having raised the number of multi-epoch systems from
\ExtSysMultiBefore{} to \ExtSysMultiAfter{} and moved
\ExtSysGained{} system out of the single-epoch class. A survey that keeps
growing has no fixed denominator for its false-alarm accounting, which is
why the analysis reported here has one. One caution carries over from the
worked case, whose two blocks are repeat executions of a single scheduling
block on one night: a second block often supplies a baseline of only
hours, and which it is can be settled with one archive query before any
download.

\emph{(3) Retain more control spectra} [repairs the second-stage null, at storage cost only] Full dynamic spectra survive for only
\LNNRetained{} of the \NCtrl{} control positions, which is what breaks the
second-stage null in a structured field and what limits the variance test of
\S\ref{sec:rsigma}. Retaining all of them costs storage and nothing else.

\emph{(4) Drift discrimination on coarse windows} [recovers an axis for \NCoarse{} of \NWindows of the windows] Within the searched
ceiling every in-grid drift on a 15.625-MHz window is sub-channel over a
track, so \NCoarse{} of \NWindows{} windows measure amplitude but cannot
distinguish a drifting carrier from a stationary one
(\S\ref{sec:dwell}). Finer channelisation, or a drift model spanning several blocks, would recover
that axis.

\emph{(5) Extend the injection campaign and the polarisation axis} [tightens the transfer bracket, the largest uncertainty on the headline number] The
stratified campaign of Appendix~\ref{app:inject} now spans band, channel
width, integration time and array, and what remains unspanned is other target
environments and edge-phased drift grids. The polarisation axis buys
discrimination against weakly polarised astrophysical backgrounds at no
sensitivity cost.

\emph{(6) A boundary on drift linearity} [bounds a modelling assumption that no observed target violates] The search assumes linear drift over a
track, and curvature matters once $\tfrac{1}{2}\ddot\nu T^{2}$ exceeds half a
native channel, which only the shortest-period orbits reach: $P\lesssim1.6$\,d
for a typical Class~A window (230\,GHz, 488\,kHz, $T\approx2000$\,s).
Among the \NExoPlanetsTab{} known planets of the \NExoHostsTab{} hosts in the
sample, \PlanetInsideN{} lies inside that boundary: \PlanetInsideName{} at
\PlanetInsideP\,d, which is also the case that reaches the drift ceiling. The
next shortest period is \PlanetNextName{} at \PlanetNextP\,d and the
shortest outside that system is \PlanetOtherName{} at \PlanetOtherP\,d, both
comfortably linear; periods are catalogued for \PlanetPerN{} of the
\NExoPlanetsTab, the exceptions being directly imaged companions on orbits of
decades, which are further from the boundary still.

The grid is built at constant acceleration rather than constant drift rate, so the boundary is one number
across the whole band: \AccelCeilMed\,m\,s$^{-2}$, which is
\AccelDriftLo\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqLo\,GHz and
\AccelDriftHi\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqHi\,GHz
(Fig.~\ref{fig:accel}). It clears the accelerations a transmitter fixed to
a rotating, orbiting planet cannot avoid by wide margins --- Earth's
rotation by a factor of \AccelRotMargin{} --- so for terrestrial-analogue
geometries the grid is not the limiting choice. The grid takes
\AccelNCeil{} values, the wider one used for the \AccelNWide{} windows of
\AccelWideStar{} alone, and even that widened grid stops
\AccelShortPct{} per cent short of \AccelWorstName{} at maximum
line-of-sight acceleration. \AccelNAbove{} of the \AccelNPlanets{} known
planets is therefore outside the searched domain and
\AccelNBelowMed{} are inside it in every window.

Disc-CO false positives should be budgeted for, but the targets should
not be pre-judged. \DiscFpWin{} of the \DiscFpOfFlags{} stage-1 flagged windows are molecular
emission toward disc hosts, arising in \DiscFpSys{} of the \NDebrisKwSys{}
debris-disc systems searched: one flagged system in \DiscFpOneIn, or
\DiscFpPerClassA{} per cent of Class~A windows. That is the natural
false-positive rate an archive proposed for disc science carries, and any
future search on ALMA, NOEMA or the JVLA inherits it. The remedy is
procedural: run the continuum lane (Appendix~\ref{app:continuum})
\emph{before} promoting a window, which would have caught HD~48370 and both
$\beta$~Pictoris windows at the first gate. We do \emph{not} recommend
lowering the threshold toward disc hosts: a detection statistic should not
know whether its target is astrophysically interesting, or a real transmitter
would be penalised for the company it keeps. Disc membership belongs in
classification, where it already is.


\section{Radio-frequency interference}
\label{app:rfi}

Nothing above rules out terrestrial interference, so this subsection
gives what does. Millimetre interferometry is an unusually poor environment for it, for three reasons. The searched range is
\RfiFreqLo--\RfiFreqHi\,GHz, above every allocated terrestrial service
and above the frequencies at which the RFI-mitigation literature of
centimetre-wave SETI operates; the array is at 5000\,m on a site chosen
partly for its radio quietness; and, decisively, an interferometer is not
a total-power detector. A signal that is not in the far field at the phase
centre does not fringe-track: it acquires a rapidly varying geometric
phase across the array and is suppressed in the correlation rather than
imaged. Three tests are applied to every stage-1 event: the spatial control
screen, which asks whether the excess is at the star or in the field; the
visibility fit of \S\ref{sec:vistest}, which asks whether it has the
phase signature of a source at the phase centre; and recurrence at the
same tuning in an independent epoch. Each is, for this reason, also an
interference test.

Three measurements support that reasoning. First, interference is a
property of the observatory and not of the star, so a terrestrial carrier
should appear at the same \emph{sky} frequency in every window that covers
it. Of the \NStageOneUnattrib{} unattributed events, \RfiNTestable{}
has a released crossing frequency; \RfiOtherStars{} windows toward
\RfiOtherStarsMin--\RfiOtherStarsMax{} other stars in \RfiOtherBlocks{}
other blocks cover the frequencies of the four, and \RfiSameChan{} of them
carries a crossing in the same channel. For the other
\RfiNNotTestable{} the pipeline stored no peak frequency, so this test
cannot be run on them and we do not claim it was. Second, over all
\RfiNCross{} released crossing frequencies in the survey there is no
clustering in sky frequency: binned at \RfiBinMHz\,MHz the largest
multiplicity is \RfiMaxMult, against \RfiNullMaxMean{} for a null that keeps each crossing in its own window and randomises its position
within it ($p=\RfiNullMaxP$). Third, an artefact of the signal chain would sit at
a fixed intermediate frequency rather than a fixed sky frequency, and the
crossings do not: their fractional positions within their own windows have
median \RfiFracMed{} and are uniform to $D=\RfiFracKsD$ at
$p=\RfiFracKsP$ over \RfiNFrac{} crossings. CP$-$72~2713's crossing,
the one with a released frequency, lies \RfiEdgeChanMin{} channels from
its nearest window edge.

None of this is a substitute for the ON/OFF cadence a dedicated search
uses, and we do not present it as one. Accordingly we claim only that
\emph{no identified interference mechanism accounts for these events};
allocation tables cannot exclude harmonics, intermodulation products,
unconventional emitters or near-field sources, and an archival dataset
without a simultaneous off-source strategy cannot do better than that. What it establishes is that the crossing population carries none of the three signatures interference
leaves: fixed sky frequency, fixed intermediate frequency, or presence in
unrelated targets. The phase test each event failed is also the test a
near-field interferer would fail.

\noindent\fbox{\parbox{0.955\columnwidth}{\footnotesize
HD~48370, in one line. $T_\star=\HdTsym$ against a ring maximum
of \HdRingMax, a margin of \HdMargin, the smallest in the survey;
CO($2{\to}1$) at $v_{\rm bary}=+41.2$\,km\,s$^{-1}$, matching the
foreground cloud \citet{Cataldi2023} report; and the visibility fit puts
the emission \emph{off} the star, with an imaginary part
\VisHdFortyIm$\sigma$ from zero. \emph{Disposition: foreground carbon monoxide}, on three
independent lines of evidence, none of them the rank.}}

Three further checks are reported in
Appendix~\ref{app:external}: a prospectively searched extension of
\ExtBlocks{} public blocks, which recovers the $\beta$~Pictoris disc CO in
three previously unsearched epochs and adds no unattributed event; the
$\beta$~Pictoris recovery itself, which is the survey's astrophysical
positive control for both the spatial and the spectral machinery; and the
absence of any shared data with the only prior ALMA-archival search, which
leaves the null result resting on internal validation alone.


